Title: It’s not just humans – chimpanzees also like to follow trends, study shows
URL: https://edition.cnn.com/2025/07/10/science/chimpanzees-grass-behavior-scli-intl
Content: # It’s not just humans – chimpanzees also like to follow trends, study shows

—

Chimpanzees living in a sanctuary in Africa have developed a “fashion trend” for dangling blades of grass or sticks from their ear holes and their behinds, a new study shows.

**********Extracted claims**********

Chimpanzees follow trends, according to a study.
Chimpanzees living in an African sanctuary developed a "fashion trend" of dangling blades of grass or sticks from their ear holes and behinds, according to a new study.
In 2010, researchers at Chimfunshi Wildlife Orphanage Trust chimpanzee sanctuary in Zambia observed a female chimpanzee dangling objects from her ear.
The behavior of dangling objects from the ear was copied by other members of the female chimpanzee's group.
There was no evidence that the chimpanzees were using the grass or sticks to deal with pain or itches.
Chimpanzees were "very relaxed" when they engaged in the dangling behavior.
The dangling behavior is considered a "fashion trend" or "social tradition."
Chimpanzees in a different group at the sanctuary started demonstrating the same behavior more than a decade later.
Some chimpanzees in the second group also inserted objects into their rectums.
The second chimpanzee group lived approximately nine miles from the first group.
The second chimpanzee group could not have copied the behavior directly from the first group.
Staff in one area of the reserve had developed a habit of cleaning their ears with matchsticks or twigs.
Staff on the other side of the reserve did not clean their ears with matchsticks or twigs.
Study lead author Ed van Leeuwen believes the behavior was picked up by chimpanzees from caregivers in the first area.
Study lead author Ed van Leeuwen believes the behavior was then passed on to other members of the chimpanzee group.
Caregivers influenced the behavior in the second chimpanzee group years later.
The second group of chimpanzees developed the practice of inserting sticks and grass into their rectums.
The behavior is a trend that spreads through social learning.
A female chimpanzee at a zoo in the Netherlands started walking as if she were carrying a baby, even though she was not.
All females in that chimpanzee group adopted the walking style.
When two new female chimpanzees were introduced to the group, the one that adopted the walking style swiftly was integrated quickly.
The new female chimpanzee who refused to adopt the group's walking style took longer to be accepted into the group.
Study lead author Ed van Leeuwen believes these chimpanzee behaviors are about fitting in.
Study lead author Ed van Leeuwen believes these chimpanzee behaviors are about smoothing social relationships.
The grass-dangling behavior was mostly observed during leisure time.
Chimpanzees engaged in the grass-dangling behavior when they congregated to groom and play.
Chimpanzees living in the sanctuary do not need to worry about predators.
Chimpanzees living in the sanctuary do not need to worry about competition with other groups.
Sanctuary chimpanzees have more leisure time than wild chimpanzees.
Study lead author Ed van Leeuwen believes wild chimpanzees are probably capable of developing such behavior.
Study lead author Ed van Leeuwen believes such behavior in wild chimpanzees might not have been documented yet.
Study lead author Ed van Leeuwen plans to study whether chimpanzees can repeatedly innovate new foraging techniques.
Study lead author Ed van Leeuwen plans to examine whether chimpanzees can develop cumulative culture similar to humans.
Elodie Freymann states that observations of this kind are key to advancing understanding of the origins and transmission patterns of cultural behaviors in chimpanzees.
Elodie Freymann states that observations of this kind are key to advancing understanding of the origins and transmission patterns of cultural behaviors in other non-human animals.
Elodie Freymann states that the study suggests there may have been interspecies copying between chimpanzees and their human caretakers.

**********Claims for hard query**********

Staff in one area of the reserve had developed a habit of cleaning their ears with matchsticks or twigs.
The dangling behavior is considered a "fashion trend" or "social tradition."
Staff on the other side of the reserve did not clean their ears with matchsticks or twigs.

