Title: Trump and his allies mount a pressure campaign against US elections ahead of the midterms
URL: https://edition.cnn.com/2025/08/02/politics/trump-elections-pressure-campaign
Content: # Trump and his allies mount a pressure campaign against US elections ahead of the midterms

A few weeks ago, Republican election officials in Colorado began receiving unsolicited calls and texts from a GOP consultant who said he was working with the Trum

**********Extracted claims**********

Republican election officials in Colorado began receiving unsolicited calls and texts from a GOP consultant.
The consultant, Jeff Small, claimed he was working with the Trump administration on “election integrity.”
Jeff Small indicated he was acting on a request from Stephen Miller, the White House deputy chief of staff.
Jeff Small told a clerk he was coordinating with the White House and the Justice Department to implement an elections executive order signed by Donald Trump.
Jeff Small requested that Colorado election officials give a third party access to their election equipment.
Justin Grantham and Carly Koppes declined Jeff Small's request.
The outreach to Colorado clerks is one of a flurry of recent federal actions launched by the Trump administration and groups aligned with Donald Trump.
The White House distanced itself from Jeff Small.
Donald Trump and his allies are collecting vast amounts of voter data.
Donald Trump and his allies are working to change the ground rules for the 2026 midterms.
These actions often involve invoking federal government authority.
Democrats need to gain three seats in the US House in 2026 to flip control of the chamber from Republicans.
Samantha Tarazi, CEO of the nonprofit Voting Rights Lab, stated she believes Donald Trump is preparing to use the power of his office to interfere in the 2026 election.
Samantha Tarazi stated that an unconstitutional executive order has grown into a full federal mobilization to seize power over elections.
White House spokesperson Harrison Fields stated that Donald Trump is fighting for election integrity.
Harrison Fields stated that Donald Trump will keep fighting for election integrity despite Democrat objections.
Harrison Fields stated that Democrat objections reveal disdain for safeguards like verifying citizenship.
Harrison Fields stated that free and fair elections are the bedrock of the Constitutional Republic.
Harrison Fields stated confidence in securing an ultimate victory in the courtroom.
Restricting who can access election machines and sensitive voting software has grown more important to election officials in recent years.
This increased importance followed voting system breaches in states such as Colorado and Georgia.
Donald Trump's allies sought access to machines to find evidence to support claims that widespread fraud marred the 2020 election.
Election watchdogs and some Democratic election officials state that activity by Donald Trump and aligned groups has raised fears of a broader effort to reshape elections.
Donald Trump signed an executive order in March that sought to force states to require proof of citizenship to register to vote.
The executive order also sought to force "enforcement action" against states that accept mail ballots after Election Day.
Federal judges have blocked parts of Donald Trump's executive order.
Federal judges noted that the power to regulate elections rests with the states and Congress, not the president.
The Republican National Committee is pushing to obtain voter registration records from states.
On the day Donald Trump signed the executive order, the Republican National Committee sent records requests to 48 states and Washington, DC.
These requests sought information on how states maintain voter registration lists.
The Republican National Committee has sued New Jersey.
The lawsuit alleges that New Jersey officials have failed to respond to requests for voter data and documents related to voting machine audits.
A spokesperson for New Jersey’s elections division declined to comment on the litigation.
The Justice Department recently asked more than a dozen states to provide voter lists.
The Justice Department also asked states to explain their procedures for removing potential ineligible voters from their rolls.
The Justice Department also asked states to discuss entering into information-sharing agreements to help the agency root out election fraud.
The Justice Department's demands range from seeking copies of voting rolls in Michigan to a broad request in Colorado for election records as far back as 2020.
Republicans in Texas are undertaking a rare mid-decade redistricting.
The Texas redistricting follows entreaties from Donald Trump.
A map released by GOP lawmakers in Texas aims to take over five additional Democratic seats.
The map would give the GOP an advantage in 30 of Texas’s 38 congressional districts.
The Republican-controlled House approved the SAVE Act in April.
The SAVE Act mirrors parts of Donald Trump’s executive order requiring proof of citizenship to register to vote.
The proposed SAVE Act would make it a crime for election officials to mistakenly register someone to vote who has not provided proof of citizenship.
Critics state that it is already illegal for noncitizens to cast ballots in federal elections.
Critics state that requiring proof of citizenship could disenfranchise eligible voters.
Texas Governor Greg Abbott justified the redistricting in his state by citing a letter from Harmeet Dhillon.
Harmeet Dhillon is the head of the Justice Department’s civil rights division.
Harmeet Dhillon's letter challenged the legality of four existing congressional districts.
Harmeet Dhillon stated that clean voter rolls and basic election safeguards are requisites for free, fair, and transparent elections.
Harmeet Dhillon stated that the agency has a statutory mandate to enforce federal voting rights laws.
Harmeet Dhillon stated that ensuring the voting public’s confidence in the integrity of elections is a top priority of the administration.
Donald Trump has been blunt about his partisan goals in Texas.
Donald Trump has suggested that other GOP-controlled states should pursue their own redistricting efforts.
The administration’s recent actions have unsettled some election officials.
Election officials have endured years of threats and harassment following the 2020 election.
Conspiracy theories about election fraud flourished after the 2020 election.
David Becker stated that election officials "are surfing on quicksand."
David Becker stated that election officials do not know what the executive order means or if it has any meaning.
David Becker stated that election officials do not know if they will be investigated for having done their jobs.
David Becker stated that election officials do not know if the vast power of the federal government is going to be weaponized against them.
David Becker stated that election officials do not know if the Department of Justice is going to be suing them.
A survey of 858 local election officials was conducted by the Brennan Center for Justice at New York University’s Law School.
59% of local election officials are concerned about political leaders engaging in efforts to interfere with how election officials do their jobs.
46% of local election officials are concerned about politically motivated investigations of their work or that of their fellow election officials.
Jeff Small worked for Colorado GOP Representative Lauren Boebert.
Jeff Small worked for the US Interior Department during Donald Trump’s first term.
Jeff Small is currently a principal with a Denver public affairs firm.
County officials stated that Jeff Small told them he was reaching out specifically to Republican clerks in blue states to help advance Donald Trump’s executive order.
Justin Grantham stated that Jeff Small’s outreach to only Republican officials was an early red flag.
Justin Grantham stated that a concern arose when Jeff Small mentioned gaining access to the county’s election equipment.
Justin Grantham stated that he did not believe the president had the constitutional authority to write executive orders to affect elections.
Justin Grantham stated that he would not let anybody access his voting equipment until the Supreme Court found the president could do so.
Suzanne Taheri, Jeff Small's attorney, responded to CNN’s inquiry.
Suzanne Taheri stated that Jeff Small’s outreach supported efforts by administration allies to encourage officials to participate in Donald Trump’s election security executive order.
Suzanne Taheri stated that Jeff Small undertook the activity on a volunteer basis, during his own free time, while on paternity leave.
Jeff Small and Suzanne Taheri did not answer questions about who in the administration asked him to contact the clerks.
A White House spokesperson stated that Jeff Small does not speak for the White House.
A White House spokesperson stated that Jeff Small was never authorized to do official business on behalf of the White House.
Stephen Miller did not respond to CNN requests for comment.
Colorado election officials report heightened sensitivity around who can access election equipment.
This heightened sensitivity followed the high-profile prosecution of former Mesa County elections clerk, Tina Peters.
Tina Peters became a celebrity among pro-Donald Trump activists.
Pro-Donald Trump activists have advanced false claims that voting machines had been rigged to flip votes in the 2020 election.
Many state laws set strict security standards for voting machines to prevent tampering with elections.
Colorado has specifically barred third parties from accessing election equipment.
A judge sentenced Tina Peters to nine years in prison.
Tina Peters was convicted on state charges for her role in a breach of her county’s election system.
The breach was part of an unsuccessful hunt for fraud.
Donald Trump and his administration have supported Tina Peters’ cause.
The Justice Department stated earlier this year that it was reviewing Tina Peters’ case.
The review is part of a broad mandate from Donald Trump to counter prosecutions.
The Justice Department stated these prosecutions were aimed more at inflicting political pain than pursuing actual justice.
Donald Trump called Tina Peters an “innocent Political Prisoner” in a social media post in May.
Donald Trump directed the Justice Department to take all necessary action to help secure Tina Peters' release.
Colorado Secretary of State Jena Griswold stated that her office has provided recent voter data to the Justice Department.
This voter data is generally available to the public.
Jena Griswold declined to comply with a Justice Department request related to records from the 2020 election.
Jena Griswold stated the federal government has no legal basis to seek 2020 election records.
Federal law only requires the preservation of election data in federal races for 22 months.
Jena Griswold stated that Donald Trump’s recent actions demonstrate he is using the power of the federal government to undermine American elections.
Jena Griswold stated that Donald Trump's actions undermine voter confidence in elections.
Donald Trump lost Colorado in all three of his White House bids.
Tensions over election administration remain high in Colorado.
Carly Koppes stated she faced threats for her outspoken defense of the 2020 election results and her county’s use of Dominion Voting machines.
Carly Koppes began varying her routes to and from work due to these threats.
The head of the clerk’s association stated it took courage for county clerks to rebuff the recent overtures.
An elections office in southern Colorado housing Dominion machines was recently firebombed.
No one was injured in the after-hours firebombing incident.
The head of the clerk’s association stated that threats against election officials are real.

