The cessation of paper checks is part of an effort to fully modernize federal benefits payments.
Approximately 0.8% of all Social Security program beneficiaries, a little over half a million recipients, must choose another way to receive their benefits.
The Social Security Administration announced in mid-July that it would no longer issue paper checks starting on September 30.
Beneficiaries who have been receiving paper checks must choose one of two alternatives: direct deposit or a pre-paid debit card called Direct Express.
The switch to electronic payments is part of a broader government-wide initiative to modernize payment systems and enhance service delivery.
The US Treasury Department stated at the end of May that all federal payments would be made electronically starting on September 30.
Federal payments include Social Security benefits, tax refunds, and vendor payments.
President Donald Trump issued an executive order to transition to all electronic payments by September 30.
Paper checks are increasingly a point of entry for fraud.
The Treasury is committed to raising awareness of growing fraud risks associated with paper checks.
The Treasury aims to provide Americans with knowledge and tools to fight financial fraud and make informed financial decisions.
Issuing paper checks costs about 50 cents each.
Sending funds electronically costs less than 15 cents.
Exceptions will be made to the policy of not sending paper checks.
Senator Elizabeth Warren stated that Social Security Commissioner Frank Bisignano committed to allowing some exceptions to the new policy.
Commissioner Bisignano stated that the plan is to start with people who have checking accounts.
Commissioner Bisignano committed that no one will be left behind and people who still need access to paper checks will have it.
A Social Security spokesperson stated that the SSA is proactively contacting beneficiaries to alert them about the change.
The Social Security spokesperson stated that the SSA is alerting beneficiaries about the process to enroll in direct deposit or receive Direct Express cards.
The Social Security spokesperson stated that paper checks will continue to be issued where a beneficiary has no other means to receive payment.
The mandate for federal payments to be made electronically has been a matter of law for years for most payments.
The law has allowed agencies to make exceptions to electronic payment mandates.
At the GoDirect site, there is a section about how recipients who still need a paper check can request a waiver.
Exceptions for paper checks are granted only in rare circumstances.
One circumstance for an exception is if electronic payments would impose a hardship due to a mental impairment.
Another circumstance for an exception is if the benefits recipient lives in a remote geographic location lacking infrastructure to support electronic financial transactions.
A third circumstance for an exception is if the recipient is age 90 or older.
People who want a waiver can contact the Waiver Line at the US Treasury Electronic Payment Solution at 1-855-290-1545 to request a waiver application.
Recipients may print out a form and mail it to the listed address to request a waiver.

