Title: ‘Dyson spheres’ were theorized as a way to detect alien life. Scientists say they’ve found potential evidence
URL: https://edition.cnn.com/2024/06/13/science/dyson-spheres-alien-life-evidence-scn
Content: # ‘Dyson spheres’ were theorized as a way to detect alien life. Scientists say they’ve found potential evidence

Sign up for CNN’s Wonder Theory science newsletter. Explore the universe with news on fascinating discoveries, scientific advancements and mor

**********Extracted claims**********

Renowned British American physicist Freeman Dyson theorized that a shell made up of mirrors or solar panels completely surrounding a star would be the ultimate solution to an advanced civilization's energy problems.
Freeman Dyson explained this concept in a 1960 paper.
In his 1960 paper, Freeman Dyson stated that an intelligent species, within a few thousand years of industrial development, would likely occupy an artificial biosphere which completely surrounds its parent star.
Freeman Dyson took the idea for Dyson spheres from Olaf Stapledon’s 1937 novel “Star Maker.”
The hypothetical megastructures designed to harness a star's energy became known as Dyson spheres.
Freeman Dyson later clarified that Dyson spheres would actually consist of a loose collection or swarm of objects traveling on independent orbits around a star.
Freeman Dyson noted in his paper that Dyson spheres would give off waste heat detectable as infrared radiation.
Freeman Dyson suggested that looking for that byproduct would be a viable method for searching for extraterrestrial life.
Freeman Dyson also stated that infrared radiation by itself would not necessarily mean extraterrestrial intelligence.
Freeman Dyson believed that one of the strongest reasons for searching for such sources was that new types of natural astronomical objects might be discovered.
Scientists in the past were largely receptive to the idea that a search for waste heat would be a good place to look for alien civilizations.
Freeman Dyson argued that technology is the only thing humans have any chance of seeing in the search for intelligence.
In the 1960s, there was no way to actually search for Dyson spheres.
In more recent times, many researchers have looked for Dyson spheres, including those at the SETI Institute and Fermi National Accelerator Laboratory (Fermilab).
The SETI Institute is a nonprofit organization with a mission to search for extraterrestrial intelligence.
A new study that looked at 5 million stars in the Milky Way galaxy suggests that seven candidates could potentially be hosting Dyson spheres.
The new study was published on May 6 in the journal Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society.
The authors of the study specifically set out to search for Dyson spheres in the form of infrared heat near stars that could not be explained in any other way.
The research team used historical data from telescopes that pick up infrared signatures.
The research team looked at stars located within 1,000 light-years from Earth.
The study started with a sample of 5 million stars.
Filters were applied to the data to reduce contamination.
Lead study author Matías Suazo stated that seven sources are known to glow in the infrared for unknown reasons.
Matías Suazo cautioned that there is no conclusive evidence that the seven stars have Dyson spheres around them.
Matías Suazo stated that it is difficult to find an explanation for these sources because there is not enough data to prove the real cause of the infrared glow.
Matías Suazo stated that the sources could be Dyson spheres because they behave like predictive models.
Matías Suazo stated that the sources could also be something else.
Natural causes that could explain the infrared glow include an unlucky alignment in the observation, with a galaxy in the background overlapping with the star.
Natural causes that could explain the infrared glow include planetary collisions creating debris.
Natural causes that could explain the infrared glow include the stars being young and still surrounded by disks of hot debris from which planets would later form.
The data used by the researchers comes from NASA’s Wide-field Infrared Survey Explorer (WISE) and the European Space Agency’s Gaia telescope.
The researchers also used data from The Two Micron All Sky Survey (2MASS).
2MASS was a collaboration between the University of Massachusetts and NASA’s Jet Propulsion Laboratory.
2MASS took place between 1997 and 2001.
The candidate stars are all red dwarfs.
Red dwarfs are the most common type of star in our galaxy.
Red dwarfs are dimmer and smaller than the Sun.
The dimness and small size of red dwarfs make follow-up observations more difficult.
It is currently unclear whether the candidate stars have planets around them.
Many thousands of exoplanets scientists have identified so far orbit red dwarfs.
According to NASA, planets orbiting red dwarfs have a higher chance of being inhabitable.
An earlier study, published in March, also found infrared anomalies among a sample dataset of 5 million stars in the galaxy.
The earlier study used data from the same sources as the new report.
Gabriella Contardo, who led the earlier study, stated her study found 53 candidates for anomalies that cannot be well explained.
Gabriella Contardo stated that not all of those 53 candidates are Dyson sphere candidates.
Gabriella Contardo stated that all other hypotheses and explanations need to be eliminated before identifying a source as a Dyson sphere.
Gabriella Contardo and Matías Suazo agree that more research is needed on the data.
NASA’s James Webb Space Telescope is powerful enough to observe the candidate stars directly.
Matías Suazo stated that having as much energy as the sun provides every second could enable interstellar travel.
Matías Suazo stated that having as much energy as the sun provides every second could enable moving an entire solar system.
The technology and the raw materials required to build hypothetical Dyson spheres are far beyond humanity’s grasp.
Matías Suazo stated that Dyson spheres are so big that everything on Earth would not be enough to build them.
Freeman Dyson suggested dismantling Jupiter, the whole planet, for the raw materials to build Dyson spheres.
The supercolossal scale of Dyson spheres probably means that, if they exist at all, they are very rare.
Study coauthor Jason Wright stated that this work provides the first strong evidence that there are not many Dyson Spheres in our galaxy.
Jason Wright stated that some had expected Dyson spheres might be an inevitable end-state of technological species’ expansion out into their solar systems.
Jason Wright stated that the candidates Matías Suazo has found are rare and interesting objects worthy of further study.
Jason Wright stated that the candidates are likely stars surrounded by material from some sort of rare event, like a planetary collision.
Jason Wright stated that the candidates could be Dyson spheres.
Freeman Dyson died in 2020.
More than one dozen ideas bear Freeman Dyson's name.
William Press stated that as a young scientist, Freeman Dyson showed that three competing quantum theories were actually the same theory.
William Press stated that Freeman Dyson applied his genius to areas of astronomy, cosmology, the extraterrestrial realm, and the problem of nuclear proliferation.
William Press stated that Freeman Dyson was recognized as a provocative and creative thinker at the time of his death.
George Dyson stated that his father, Freeman Dyson, contributed to five fields of mathematics and eleven fields of physics.
George Dyson stated that Freeman Dyson also contributed to theoretical biology, engineering, operations research, literature, and public affairs.
Many of Freeman Dyson's ideas were controversial.
One of Freeman Dyson's guiding principles was "It is better to be wrong than to be vague."
Tomotsugu Goto stated that the approach of the researchers behind the new study could offer a more fruitful path in the search for extraterrestrial intelligence.
Tomotsugu Goto stated that contamination by circumstellar debris disks, which mimic Dyson Sphere infrared signatures, remains a concern.
Tomotsugu Goto noted that the study authors argue that debris disks around dwarf stars are rare.
Tomotsugu Goto noted that the seven candidate sources selected out of 5 million are also rare.
Tomotsugu Goto stated that the seven candidates warrant further investigation with powerful telescopes for a more definitive evaluation.
A May 23 paper, published in response to the one by Suazo and his colleagues, suggests that at least three of the seven stars have been "misidentified" as Dyson spheres.
The May 23 paper suggests these misidentified stars could instead be "hot DOGs," which are hot dust-obscured galaxies.
The May 23 paper suggests that the remaining four candidates could probably be explained as "hot DOGs" as well.
Zaza Osmanov stated that the search for Dyson candidates straddles different fields, including basic sciences, philosophy, and religion.
Zaza Osmanov stated that the search for Dyson candidates might therefore increase the involvement of young scientists and the interest of the scientific world in the topic.
Zaza Osmanov stated that the radiation fingerprint of the seven Dyson sphere candidates might be explained by natural phenomena.
Zaza Osmanov stated that the hypothesis of artificial origin of any radiation should be the last consideration, when all possible natural explanations are exhausted.
Zaza Osmanov stated that future research is necessary for evaluating the origin of the radiation.
George Dyson stated that his father, Freeman Dyson, would have been highly skeptical that these observations represent a technological signature.
George Dyson stated that Freeman Dyson believed the discovery of new, non-technological astronomical phenomena was exactly why people should look for such observations.

