Title: 4,000-year-old teeth reveal the earliest use of this psychoactive substance
URL: https://edition.cnn.com/2025/07/31/science/thailand-betel-chewing-neolithic-archaeology-intl-hnk
Content: # 4,000-year-old teeth reveal the earliest use of this psychoactive substance

For the first time, archaeologists have used advanced scientific techniques on 4,000-year-old dental plaque to confirm traces of betel nut chewing in ancient Thai communities.


**********Extracted claims**********

Archaeologists have used advanced scientific techniques on 4,000-year-old dental plaque to confirm traces of betel nut chewing in ancient Thai communities for the first time.
Betel nuts are typically chewed as "quids."
A "quid" consists of a mix of slaked lime and ground betel nuts, wrapped in a betel leaf.
Betel nuts contain psychoactive compounds.
These psychoactive compounds boost energy, alertness, euphoria, and relaxation.
The stimulant from betel nut chewing can leave red, brown, or black stains on the teeth.
Betel nut is considered the world's fourth most commonly used psychoactive substance.
Caffeine, alcohol, and nicotine are the three most commonly used psychoactive substances globally.
Hundreds of millions of people globally use betel nut.
Betel nuts have been identified at archaeological sites via plant fragments.
Betel nuts have been identified at archaeological sites via stained teeth.
Past archaeological identifications provided circumstantial evidence that betel nut use dates back at least 8,000 years.
An international team of researchers identified betel nut chewing in an individual with no dental discoloration using advanced scientific techniques.
The study, published in Frontiers in Environmental Archaeology, provides the earliest direct biochemical proof of betel nut consumption in Southeast Asia.
The evidence from the study predates previous evidence of betel nut consumption by at least 1,000 years.
Piyawit Moonkham is an archaeologist at Chiang Mai University in Thailand and an author of the study.
The discovery of “invisible” traces of betel nut chewing in molars suggests that visible evidence might not reveal the complete story for some prehistoric practices.
The method used in the study is highly sensitive.
The method used in the study is minimally invasive.
The method used in the study requires only tiny samples of plaque.
The method offers a way of finding more clues about the past.
Thanik Lertcharnrit is an associate professor at Silpakorn University in Bangkok, Thailand.
Thanik Lertcharnrit is an expert in Southeast Asian archaeology.
Few archaeologists use residue analysis to infer the life, tradition, or culture of prehistoric people.
The paper represents pioneering archaeological research.
The paper’s methodology is considered state-of-the-art in archaeological research in mainland South Asia, particularly in Thailand.
Researchers began collecting ancient dental plaque, also known as calculus, from Nong Ratchawat in 2021.
Nong Ratchawat is a Neolithic burial site located in central Thailand.
The research team removed tiny, five-milligram scrapes of plaque from 36 dental samples.
The 36 dental samples were taken from six individuals.
The method used is called liquid chromatography-mass spectrometry (LC-MS).
LC-MS extracts, separates, and identifies chemical compounds.
LC-MS identifies chemical compounds by measuring a molecule's weight compared to its electrical charge.
The research team used control samples to demonstrate what traces of betel nut liquid might look like before testing ancient samples.
Control samples included dried betel nut, red limestone paste, and piper betel leaves as core ingredients.
Some control samples also included catechu bark and tobacco.
The ingredients in the control samples were ground together with human saliva.
Modern control samples were tested first to validate the method.
Trace plant alkaloids, including arecoline and arecaidine, were detected in three samples from one individual known as “Burial 11.”
Arecoline and arecaidine are betel nut’s main psychoactive compounds.
Individual “Burial 11” was likely a woman aged around 25.
The technique used in the study does not destroy the original samples.
The technique leaves remains intact for future study.
LC-MS is currently used in pharmaceuticals, food safety, and environmental testing.
The use of LC-MS in archaeology has been limited.
Dr. Melandri Vlok is a bioarchaeologist.
Dr. Melandri Vlok is a lecturer in anatomy and physiology at Charles Sturt University in Australia.
Much work using the LC-MS method has focused on finding proteins in dental calculus for dietary reasons.
Using LC-MS to detect compounds trapped in dental plaque is innovative.
This specific application of LC-MS to detect betel nut compounds in plaque had not been done before the study.
The LC-MS method requires expensive machinery.
An Orbitrap is an advanced mass spectrometer.
An Orbitrap identifies molecules by measuring their mass-to-charge ratio.
Many researchers do not have access to the required expensive machinery.
LC-MS is becoming more routinely used by larger labs, such as Harvard and Max Planck.
The paper has a Thai first-author.
The research team included researchers from eight institutions across three continents.
The chemical residue analysis was performed at Washington State University.
Piyawit Moonkham studied for his PhD at Washington State University.
The study’s control samples are considered a novelty.
Control samples created a "standard" for testing.
Future studies could refine the method by considering how compounds degrade over thousands of years.
The method is likely to be used frequently in the region in the future.
Betel nuts have historically been linked to hospitality.
Betel nuts have historically been linked to religious rituals.
Recent research focuses on betel nut’s classification as a carcinogen.
Recent research focuses on the correlation between betel quid use and oral cancers.
Betel nut chewing has significant implications for human health.
Betel nut chewing affects millions of people in tropical Asia-Pacific.
The Thai government has strongly discouraged betel nut practice since the 1940s.
Betel nut chewing remains popular in rural areas of Thailand.
Betel nut chewing is uncommon in Thai cities.
Betel nut chewing is uncommon among younger generations in Thailand.
Piyawit Moonkham believes the practice of betel nut chewing has been overly “demonized.”
Piyawit Moonkham hopes the research can illustrate the long history of betel nuts in Thailand.
Piyawit Moonkham hopes the research can illustrate the importance of betel nuts in Thai society.
Piyawit Moonkham’s grandparents regularly chewed betel quids.
Piyawit Moonkham’s grandparents often chewed betel quids while gossiping with friends or relaxing after a family meal.
Piyawit Moonkham’s grandmother stated that chewing betel nuts cleans teeth.
Piyawit Moonkham’s grandmother stated that chewing betel nuts helps her relax.
Piyawit Moonkham’s grandmother shared betel nuts with friends, family, or colleagues while chewing.
Piyawit Moonkham believes betel nut chewing creates a social bond.
Researchers are exploring reasons for the absence of tooth stains in the individual examined in the study.
Possible reasons for the absence of tooth stains include different chewing methods, cleaning habits, or decay over thousands of years.
The research team plans to analyze more individuals from the Nong Ratchawat site.
An additional 150 individuals from the Nong Ratchawat site could be tested for betel nut use.
Piyawit Moonkham intends to investigate the social, religious, and medicinal roles of betel nut in ancient societies in future projects.
The technique used could be applied to a wide range of plant residues.
The technique used could be applied to a wide range of food residues.
Applying the technique to plant and food residues could open new avenues for understanding ancient practices.

