Title: You can slow cognitive decline as you age, large study finds. Here’s how
URL: https://edition.cnn.com/2025/07/28/health/cognitive-decline-pointer-study-wellness
Content: # You can slow cognitive decline as you age, large study finds. Here’s how

Video 
\- Source:

At 62, Phyllis Jones felt trapped in darkness. She was traumatized by her mother’s recent death, ongoing pandemic stress and an increasingly toxic work environm

**********Extracted claims**********

The Protect Brain Health Through Lifestyle Intervention to Reduce Risk is also known as the US POINTER study.
The US POINTER study is the largest randomized clinical trial in the United States.
The US POINTER study was designed to examine whether lifestyle interventions can protect cognitive function in older adults.
Participants in the US POINTER study were cognitively healthy individuals aged 60 to 79.
Participants in the US POINTER study had to be completely sedentary and at risk for dementia due to health issues.
Health issues putting participants at risk for dementia included prediabetes and borderline high blood pressure.
Laura Baker is the principal investigator of the US POINTER study.
Laura Baker is a professor of gerontology, geriatrics, and internal medicine at Wake Forest University School of Medicine in Winston-Salem, North Carolina.
The US POINTER study had 2,111 participants.
Approximately half of the US POINTER study participants attended 38 structured team meetings over two years.
Structured team meetings were held in local neighborhoods near Chicago, Houston, Winston-Salem, Sacramento, California, and Providence, Rhode Island.
Trained facilitators provided guidance on exercise, brain-healthy eating, socialization, brain-training games, and brain health basics during structured sessions.
Team leaders held structured groups accountable for logging blood pressure and other vitals.
Physical and cognitive exams were conducted by a physician every six months for all study participants.
The other half of study participants attended six team meetings and formed a self-guided group.
The self-guided group learned about brain health and selected lifestyle changes without goal-directed coaching.
The US POINTER study lasted two years.
The US POINTER study cost $50 million.
The US POINTER study was funded by the Alzheimer’s Association.
The two-year results of the US POINTER study were presented at the 2025 Alzheimer’s Association International Conference in Toronto.
The two-year results of the US POINTER study were published in the journal JAMA.
Participants in the structured program appeared to delay normal cognitive aging by one to nearly two years more than the self-guided group.
The self-guided group also improved their cognitive scores over time.
Participants in the study received YMCA memberships and lessons on how to use gym equipment.
Participants were advised to use aerobic exercise to raise their heart rate for 30 minutes a day and to add strength training and stretching several times a week.
Study participants wore fitness trackers to monitor their activity.
The Mediterranean-DASH Intervention for Neurodegenerative Delay (MIND) diet was introduced to participants.
The MIND diet combines elements of the Mediterranean diet and the Dietary Approaches to Stop Hypertension (DASH) diet.
The MIND diet required eating berries and vegetables most days, including green leafy vegetables as a separate item.
The MIND diet required consuming 2 tablespoons of extra-virgin olive oil once every day.
The MIND diet limited fried food, processed meat, dairy, cheese, butter, and sugary sweets.
Participants on the MIND diet were allowed dessert four times a week.
The program required participants to familiarize themselves with their vital signs and encouraged monitoring blood sugar.
Brain training was provided through memberships to a Web-based cognitive training app.
Socializing was a key part of the program, with teams given assignments related to it.
Researchers reduced the frequency of check-ins as the study progressed.
Researchers closely tracked each team in the study.
The US POINTER study has a wealth of unmined data.
The data collected includes information on exercise, brain training engagement, MIND diet scores, meeting attendance, sleep, blood biomarkers, and brain scans.
The US POINTER study has limitations, including the potential for a phenomenon known as the practice effect.
Repeated testing can lead to familiarity with the testing situation and improved test performance due to increased comfort.
Both groups in the US POINTER study appeared to have improved their global cognition.
Global cognition includes thinking, learning, and problem-solving.
Interpretations of the US POINTER study results require caution due to potential limitations.
The POINTER study was not designed to provide immersive lifestyle interventions for people with early stages of Alzheimer’s.
Dean Ornish is a professor of medicine at the University of California, San Francisco.
Dean Ornish published a clinical trial in June 2024.
Ornish's trial found that a strict vegan diet, daily exercise, structured stress reduction, and frequent socialization could stop or improve cognitive decline in people with early-stage Alzheimer’s disease.
The US POINTER randomized clinical trial is a landmark study.
The US POINTER study shows that moderate lifestyle changes, including diet, exercise, and socialization, can improve cognition in people at risk for dementia.
Dean Ornish is the creator of the Ornish diet and lifestyle medicine program.
Dean Ornish is a coauthor of “Undo It!: How Simple Lifestyle Changes Can Reverse Most Chronic Diseases.”
More intensive multiple lifestyle changes often improve cognition in those diagnosed with early-stage Alzheimer’s disease.
The US POINTER study suggests that more moderate lifestyle changes may be sufficient to help prevent cognitive decline.
Lifestyle interventions can help delay cognitive aging.
Lifestyle interventions include exercise, a healthy diet, an active social life, and managing other health issues.
Two years is not sufficient to track brain changes over time.
Maria Carillo is a study coauthor.
Maria Carillo is the chief science officer of the Alzheimer’s Association.
An additional $40 million has been invested in a four-year follow-up to the US POINTER study.
Over 80% of the original participants have joined the four-year follow-up study.
Brain health is difficult to track over short periods.

