Content: # The Trump administration takes a very Orwellian turn

Back in March, President Donald Trump signed an executive order targeted at the Smithsonian Institution that began as follows: “Over the past decade, Americans have witnessed a concerted and widespre

**********Extracted claims**********

President Donald Trump signed an executive order targeted at the Smithsonian Institution in March.
The executive order stated that Americans had witnessed a concerted and widespread effort to rewrite the Nation’s history over the past decade.
The executive order stated that this effort replaced objective facts with a distorted narrative driven by ideology rather than truth.
The executive order cited a desire to remove “improper ideology” from properties like the Smithsonian.
Historical information, specifically a reference to Donald Trump's two impeachments, vanished from the Smithsonian.
The vanished information was objective history that Donald Trump reportedly disliked.
The Smithsonian stated that a board containing the information about Donald Trump's impeachments was removed from the National Museum of American History.
The board's removal occurred after a review of the museum’s “legacy content.”
The board had been placed in front of an existing impeachment exhibit in September 2021.
The exhibit is titled “Limits of Presidential Power.”
The Washington Post reported that a source stated the content review at the Smithsonian followed pressure from the White House to remove an art museum director.
The Smithsonian stated in a statement on Saturday that it was not asked by any administration or government official to remove content.
The Smithsonian stated that an updated version of the exhibit will ultimately mention all impeachment efforts, including Donald Trump’s.
Donald Trump fired the commissioner of the Bureau of Labor Statistics on Friday.
The firing occurred hours after the Bureau of Labor Statistics delivered job numbers that were the worst non-Covid three-month jobs numbers since 2010.
Donald Trump told Newsmax that the commissioner was fired because he did not believe the numbers.
The commissioner fired was Dr. Erika McEntarfer.
CNN’s Daniel Dale documented on Friday that any evidence-based case Donald Trump presented for firing Dr. McEntarfer was conspiratorial and wrong.
Senator Cynthia Lummis of Wyoming called the firing of the Bureau of Labor Statistics head “kind of impetuous.”
Senator Rand Paul of Kentucky expressed worry that Donald Trump’s move would cause people to distrust the data the administration is releasing.
Senator Lisa Murkowski of Alaska expressed worry that Donald Trump’s move would cause people to distrust the data the administration is releasing.
Donald Trump engaged in an effort to portray January 6 defendants as sympathetic patriots.
Donald Trump called January 6 defendants “hostages.”
Donald Trump pardoned January 6 defendants.
The Trump administration’s efforts to remove diversity, equity, and inclusion initiatives from the government included actions against things that celebrated Black people and women.
Donald Trump and his administration have at times viewed free speech rights of those who disagree with them unfavorably.
Donald Trump and his administration have referred to protests as “illegal.”
A loyalist US attorney at one point threatened to pursue people who criticized Elon Musk, even for non-criminal behavior.
Donald Trump has repeatedly suggested that criticism of judges he favors should be illegal.
Donald Trump’s term began with the removal of portraits of military leaders who clashed with him from the Pentagon.
Donald Trump’s term also began with a significant removal of independent inspectors general.
Independent inspectors general are charged with holding the administration to account.

