Title: Roman-era ‘church’ in Spain may have been a synagogue
URL: https://edition.cnn.com/2025/07/30/science/synagogue-castulo-spain-scli-intl
Content: # Roman-era ‘church’ in Spain may have been a synagogue

Archaeologists working at a site in Spain say they have uncovered evidence of what may have been a synagogue used by a hitherto unknown Jewish community.

While excavating the site, previously belie

**********Extracted claims**********

Archaeologists are working at a site in Spain.
Evidence has been uncovered at the Spanish site.
The site was previously believed to be a church.
The previously believed church dated from the 4th century.
Experts found materials and architectural evidence during excavation at the site.
A study about the findings was published.
Artifacts were found during excavations in Cástulo.
Cástulo is a former Roman settlement in southern Spain.
Fragments of oil lamps were found at the Cástulo site.
A piece of roof tile decorated with menorahs was found at the Cástulo site.
No materials with a clear association with the Christian faith have been found at the Cástulo site.
Archaeologists have found evidence of Christian worship at another site in Cástulo.
Bautista Ceprián is a study author.
Bautista Ceprián is an archaeologist with the Cástulo Sefarad Primera Luz project.
The building at the site has a squarer shape.
Christian churches tend to be more rectangular.
Archaeologists found a hole in the building that could have supported a large menorah.
Archaeologists found the foundations of a central raised platform in the building.
A central raised platform is known as a bimah.
A bimah is common in synagogues.
A bimah is not common in churches.
No tombs were discovered at the building.
The building was built near an abandoned Roman temple.
Christian residents would have feared the association of abandoned Roman temples with paganism.
The site is a hidden, discreet, and isolated spot.
The reinterpretation of the building from a church to a possible synagogue followed a process of logical reasoning.
The reasoning for reinterpretation was based on historical and archaeological data.
There is a lack of written records of a Jewish community in Cástulo.
The study authors acknowledged that the lack of written records leaves room for doubt regarding the synagogue hypothesis.
The Cástulo population is thought to have disappeared.
The Cástulo population is not named in the anti-Jewish law enacted by Visigoth King Sisebut.
Visigoth King Sisebut ruled what is now Spain from 612 to 621.
Jewish communities in other nearby towns are specifically named in the anti-Jewish law enacted by Visigoth King Sisebut.
It is difficult to know what happened to the Cástulo population.
One possible explanation for the population's disappearance is that Christian clergy feared the local population would convert to Judaism.
Close and friendly relations existed between Christian and Jewish groups in the region at the time.
Christian leaders were becoming increasingly influential in the Roman Empire.
Episodes started around the end of the 4th century.
In these episodes, Jewish citizens were pressured to convert to Christianity.
Jewish citizens who refused conversion were invited to leave their hometowns in these episodes.

