Title: ‘It’s just PR’: Skittles, Hershey and Nestle are removing artificial colors
URL: https://edition.cnn.com/2025/08/04/business/maha-rfk-food-artificial-colors
Content: # ‘It’s just PR’: Skittles, Hershey and Nestle are removing artificial colors

—

The Trump administration declared victory after Kraft Heinz, Skittles and General Mills made splashy announcements to remove artificial colors — even taking credit for Coca-

**********Extracted claims**********

The Trump administration declared victory after Kraft Heinz, Skittles, and General Mills announced the removal of artificial colors.
The Trump administration took credit for Coca-Cola's plan to replace high-fructose corn syrup with US cane sugar in a new version of its product.
The White House stated that President Trump delivers on the MAHA (Make America Healthy Again) push.
The White House touted companies' changes as confronting the chronic health crisis plaguing Americans.
Nutritionists and public health researchers state that companies have made only performative changes.
Nutritionists and public health researchers state that many company changes were long in the works due to consumer demand for natural ingredients.
Dr. Barry Popkin, a professor of nutrition at the University of North Carolina, states that company changes are cosmetic with no health impact.
Dr. Barry Popkin states that the changes allow MAHA proponents to claim a victory.
Health advocates credit Robert F. Kennedy Jr. for bringing attention to unhealthy foods, chronic disease, and major companies’ influence on the food system in America.
Robert F. Kennedy Jr. is the Health and Human Services Department secretary.
Researchers state that Robert F. Kennedy Jr.’s focus on synthetic dyes overlooks the issue of the prevalence of cheap, convenient foods high in salt, sugar, and fat.
Dr. Popkin stated that ice cream and soda remain the same even without artificial flavors.
An HHS spokesperson stated that Robert F. Kennedy Jr. is dismantling the status quo that fueled a nationwide chronic disease epidemic.
An HHS spokesperson stated that Robert F. Kennedy Jr. is eliminating bureaucracy and restoring integrity to federal health programs.
An HHS spokesperson stated that HHS is confronting the root causes of chronic illness.
Researchers state that implementing policies to tightly regulate ultraprocessed foods is necessary for the administration to make a substantive impact on Americans’ diets.
Ultraprocessed foods constitute up to 70% of the US food supply.
Ultraprocessed foods include popular brands of chips, cookies, candy, ice cream, and pre-made meals.
Studies have frequently linked ultraprocessed foods to obesity, cancer, diabetes, heart disease, and other health disorders.
Robert F. Kennedy Jr. has called the easy availability of ultraprocessed foods a crisis.
Robert F. Kennedy Jr. oversaw the White House’s Make America Healthy Again Commission report.
The White House’s Make America Healthy Again Commission report was released in May.
The White House’s Make America Healthy Again Commission report identifies ultraprocessed foods as a key contributor to a national rise in chronic illnesses.
The report particularly highlights the rise in chronic illnesses among children.
The Health and Human Services Department has not yet enacted significant measures to reduce ultraprocessed foods.
Dr. Popkin stated that the administration is not currently targeting the real food culprit.
Dr. Popkin stated that significant action by Robert F. Kennedy Jr. on ultraprocessed foods would be hugely important for health.
Synthetic dyes are made from petroleum.
Synthetic dyes are often used to make food and beverages brightly colored and appealing to customers, especially children.
Synthetic dyes have potential negative effects on animal and human health.
Potential negative effects of synthetic dyes include a possible increased risk of cancer.
Potential negative effects of synthetic dyes include neurobehavioral issues in some children.
The US Food and Drug Administration banned red dye No. 3 in food, beverages, and ingested drugs in January.
Robert F. Kennedy Jr. has been pressuring food companies to voluntarily remove all food dyes from their products.
Many food companies have been moving away from synthetic dyes for years.
This shift is due to pressure from consumers and health advocates.
This shift is also due to bans or restrictions in states such as California, Virginia, and West Virginia.
Kraft Heinz and General Mills have already removed synthetic color additives from most of their products.
The administration recently celebrated Kraft Heinz and General Mills for pledging to remove synthetic colors.
Dr. Marion Nestle, an emeritus professor of nutrition and food studies at New York University, stated that removing artificial colors is nutritionally meaningless.
Dr. Marion Nestle stated that removing artificial colors gives compliant junk foods the aura of health foods.
Roughly 64% of consumers actively look for snacks perceived as “good for them.”
This figure has increased sharply.
Market research firm Circana provided this information.
Yogurt, cheeses, and foods/drinks with protein have proliferated.
Their proliferation is due to their nutritional appeal.
Companies have previously made voluntary pledges to remove artificial dyes from their products.
Many companies have backtracked on their commitments regarding artificial dyes.
Aviva Musicus, science director at the Center for Science in the Public Interest, hopes industry will voluntarily improve the food supply.
The Center for Science in the Public Interest is a nonprofit consumer advocacy group.
The Consumer Brands Association stated in April that the industry has always prioritized transparency.
The Consumer Brands Association stated that the industry will continue to lead to ensure consumers have needed information for informed purchasing decisions.
The Consumer Brands Association stated that defining foods as "ultraprocessed" may demonize safe, shelf-ready foods.
The Consumer Brands Association stated that defining foods as "ultraprocessed" may limit consumers’ access to nutritious foods.
A Congressional Budget Office estimate predicts that Trump’s tax and spending cuts package will result in 10 million more people without health insurance in 2034.
The Urban Institute states that over 22 million families will lose some or all of their Supplemental Nutrition Assistance Program (SNAP) benefits.
The law eliminated funding for programs offering cooking classes and nutrition education for SNAP recipients.
The Agriculture Department cut two pandemic-era programs.
The cut programs helped schools and food banks buy from local farmers.
Aviva Musicus stated that the federal government cut SNAP benefits for millions of Americans.
Aviva Musicus stated that millions of Americans lost health insurance coverage.
Aviva Musicus stated that programs helping farmers bring local foods into schools were slashed.
The administration slashed billions in research funding.
The administration fired thousands of employees at the National Institutes of Health (NIH), Centers for Disease Control (CDC), and other agencies.
The top nutrition researcher at NIH, who focused on ultraprocessed foods, left the agency.
The researcher cited censorship under Robert F. Kennedy Jr. as the reason for leaving.
HHS previously denied the claims of censorship.
Dr. Jerold Mande, an adjunct professor at the Harvard School of Public Health, stated that research cuts and layoffs may hinder the enactment of stricter rules.
Dr. Jerold Mande stated that research cuts and layoffs may hinder investigations of the food industry.
Dr. Jerold Mande was a senior policy official in the Obama and Clinton administrations.
Dr. Jerold Mande stated that tens of thousands of government employees needed for investigations have been lost.
Dr. Mande credits Robert F. Kennedy Jr. for making chronic disease caused by food, principally obesity, a political priority.
The MAHA Commission will release its second report on the strategy for improving childhood chronic obesity in August.

