Title: OPEC+ makes another large oil output hike in market share push
URL: https://edition.cnn.com/2025/08/03/business/oil-prices-market-opec
Content: # OPEC+ makes another large oil output hike in market share push

OPEC+ agreed on Sunday to raise oil production by 547,000 barrels per day for September, the latest in a series of accelerated output hikes to regain market share, as concerns mount over po

**********Extracted claims**********

OPEC+ agreed to increase oil production by 547,000 barrels per day for September.
The increase in oil production by OPEC+ is part of a series of accelerated output hikes.
OPEC+ aims to regain market share through increased oil output.
Concerns exist over potential supply disruptions related to Russia.
OPEC+ has fully and early reversed its largest tranche of output cuts.
A separate increase in output for the United Arab Emirates amounts to about 2.5 million barrels per day.
2.5 million barrels per day represents approximately 2.4% of world demand.
Eight OPEC+ members held a virtual meeting.
The United States is pressuring India to stop purchasing Russian oil.
U.S. efforts aim to bring Moscow to the negotiating table for a peace deal with Ukraine.
President Donald Trump expressed a desire for a peace deal by August 8.
OPEC+ cited a healthy economy as a reason for its decision to raise output.
OPEC+ cited low stocks as a reason for its decision to raise output.
Oil prices have remained elevated despite OPEC+ raising output.
Brent crude closed near $70 a barrel on Friday.
Brent crude was near $58 in April 2025.
Rising seasonal demand supports oil prices.
Amrita Sen, co-founder of Energy Aspects, stated that strong oil prices give OPEC+ confidence about market fundamentals.
Amrita Sen also stated that the market structure indicates tight stocks.
The eight OPEC+ countries are scheduled to meet again on September 7.
OPEC+ sources stated that the eight countries may consider reinstating about 1.65 million barrels per day of output cuts.
Output cuts totaling around 1.65 million barrels per day are currently in place until the end of next year.
OPEC+ includes 10 non-OPEC oil producing countries.
Russia and Kazakhstan are notable non-OPEC oil producing countries within OPEC+.
The OPEC+ group pumps about half of the world's oil.
The OPEC+ group had been curtailing production for several years to support oil prices.
OPEC+ reversed its course on production curtailment to regain market share.
Calls from President Trump for OPEC to ramp up production partly spurred the reversal.
The eight members of OPEC+ began raising output in April with a 138,000 barrels per day hike.
Larger-than-planned hikes of 411,000 barrels per day occurred in May, June, and July.
A 548,000 barrels per day hike occurred in August.
Giovanni Staunovo of UBS stated that the market has absorbed the additional barrels well.
Stockpiling activity in China contributed to the market absorbing additional barrels.
OPEC+ still has a 2-million-barrel-per-day cut across all members.
The 2-million-barrel-per-day cut expires at the end of 2026.
Jorge Leon of Rystad Energy stated that OPEC+ has passed its first test by reversing its largest cut without crashing prices.
Jorge Leon stated that the next task for OPEC+ will be harder.
The next task for OPEC+ involves deciding if and when to unwind the remaining 1.66 million barrels per day of cuts.
The next task for OPEC+ requires navigating geopolitical tension.
The next task for OPEC+ requires preserving cohesion among members.

