Title: Disrupted US vaccine meetings could threaten timelines, access and transparency around shots
URL: https://edition.cnn.com/2025/02/28/health/vaccine-meetings-rfk-flu-shots
Content: # Disrupted US vaccine meetings could threaten timelines, access and transparency around shots

—

Two long-planned vaccine meetings — one at the US Centers for Disease Control and another at the US Food and Drug Administration — have now been disrupted u

**********Extracted claims**********

Two long-planned vaccine meetings, one at the US Centers for Disease Control (CDC) and another at the US Food and Drug Administration (FDA), have been disrupted.
The February meeting of the CDC’s Advisory Committee on Immunization Practices (ACIP) was postponed.
The postponed ACIP meeting was scheduled to review the safety and effectiveness of vaccines for meningococcal disease, RSV, flu, chikungunya, and mpox.
The postponed ACIP meeting included two votes related to Vaccines for Children.
Vaccines for Children is a government program that buys and distributes vaccines to state and local health departments for children whose families would not otherwise be able to pay for them.
ACIP typically meets three times a year, in February, June, and October.
The FDA canceled a March 13 meeting of its Vaccines and Related Biological Products Advisory Committee (VRBPAC).
The canceled VRBPAC meeting was scheduled to discuss the recipe manufacturers must follow to make fall flu shots.
No explanation was provided for the cancellation of the FDA's VRBPAC meeting.
The FDA stated it would still make recommendations on the makeup of flu shots.
The FDA did not indicate whether it would seek its advisers’ guidance or allow for public discussion and debate when making recommendations on flu shot makeup.
The FDA stated it would make its recommendations to manufacturers public in time for updated vaccines for the 2025-2026 influenza season.
The US is currently experiencing a severe flu season.
Measles, a vaccine-preventable disease, is experiencing a resurgence in the US.
Experts state that infections are increasing due to growing vaccine hesitancy.
Robert F. Kennedy Jr. maintains he is not anti-vaccine.
Robert F. Kennedy Jr. has repeatedly questioned the safety and effectiveness of immunizations.
Robert F. Kennedy Jr. has repeatedly questioned the research and regulations concerning immunizations.
Robert F. Kennedy Jr.'s financial disclosures indicate he has earned money from law firms that sue vaccine manufacturers.
During his confirmation hearings, Robert F. Kennedy Jr. incorrectly stated that 97% of the CDC’s vaccine advisory committee members had conflicts of interest.
The CDC’s vaccine advisory committee has strict policies to prevent conflicts of interest.
The CDC’s vaccine advisory committee has a screening process for members to ensure impartiality during voting.
Members of the FDA’s vaccine committee are subject to strict conflict of interest rules.
Members of the FDA’s vaccine committee are required to disclose potential conflicts during meetings.
Former CDC Director Dr. Tom Frieden stated that false claims about ACIP members voting based on financial ties are among the most damaging falsehoods about vaccines.
Dr. Tom Frieden stated the 97% figure originated from a 2009 report.
Dr. Tom Frieden is the president and CEO of the nonprofit Resolve to Save Lives.
The 97% figure referred to an audit of conflict of interest forms submitted by CDC advisers in 2007.
Dr. Tom Frieden stated that 97% of the audited forms had at least one missing response.
Dr. Tom Frieden stated that a missing response is not equivalent to a conflict of interest.
Dr. Tom Frieden raised concerns about the postponed ACIP meeting.
ACIP is comprised of pediatricians, public health specialists, and parents.
ACIP is considered the gold standard for open and transparent recommendations on vaccine safety and effectiveness, according to Dr. Tom Frieden.
Healthcare providers express concern that delays or limited access to vaccines may occur without the vaccine committees' input.
Dr. Tina Tan, president of the Infectious Diseases Society of America, stated that canceling the FDA meeting may prevent vaccine makers from having vital information and time to produce and distribute targeted vaccines before the next flu season.
A coalition of groups called the Partnership to Fight Infectious Disease, along with Acting CDC Director Dr. Susan Monarez and Senator Bill Cassidy, urged the prompt rescheduling of the ACIP meeting in an open letter.
The letter from the Partnership to Fight Infectious Disease stated that each ACIP meeting holds tremendous weight and relevance.
The letter from the Partnership to Fight Infectious Disease stated that vaccines are among the best tools for adapting to public health threats.
The most common method to make flu vaccines involves growing selected candidate viruses in chicken eggs.
Growing selected candidate viruses in chicken eggs for flu vaccines is a time-consuming operation.
The selection of flu vaccine strains is carefully orchestrated to ensure timely delivery to manufacturers.
Flu vaccines sold in the US must contain strains officially selected by the FDA.
The FDA's flu strain selection decision typically follows a meeting of the World Health Organization’s Global Influenza Surveillance and Response System.
The World Health Organization’s Global Influenza Surveillance and Response System is a network of seven collaborating centers and four essential regulatory labs.
The centers and labs of the Global Influenza Surveillance and Response System are located in the US, UK, Japan, China, Russia, and Australia.
Both the CDC and FDA are members of the World Health Organization’s Global Influenza Surveillance and Response System.
Representatives from the CDC and FDA participated in the Global Influenza Surveillance and Response System deliberations.
Dr. Maria Van Kerkhove, WHO’s technical lead for Covid-19, stated that the US participated in a recent meeting.
Experts within the Global Influenza Surveillance and Response System meet twice a year to choose flu strains for the Southern and Northern Hemispheres.
The WHO announced its recommended flu strains.
The WHO's recommended strains involve replacing the H3N2 component from the previous year's vaccine.
The 17-member FDA vaccine committee typically meets to publicly discuss and endorse flu strain recommendations.
The FDA makes the final decision on flu strains.
The FDA's final decision on flu strains can differ from its advisers' recommendations, though they generally align.
There will be no FDA advisory meeting on flu strains this year.
Dr. LJ Tan of Immunize.org stated that without FDA-recommended strains, US manufacturers would face difficulties.
Flu vaccine manufacturers stated the canceled VRBPAC meeting would not disrupt their production.
Sanofi stated it has begun production for the 2025-2026 flu season and will be ready for final strain selections.
GSK stated it will continue to collaborate with the FDA on its flu strain recommendation in the US.
Dr. LJ Tan of Immunize.org stated that canceling the VRBPAC meeting reduces transparency and public discussion.
In his Senate confirmation hearings, Robert F. Kennedy Jr. promised "radical transparency" at HHS.
Vaccine advocates state that canceling advisory committee meetings undermines the goal of transparency.
Dr. Stanley Perlman, a member of FDA’s VRBPAC, stated that advisory committee meetings allow the public to see how conclusions are reached.
Dr. Stanley Perlman expressed concern that skipping committee meetings would reduce public insight into government vaccine decisions.
Experts agree that advisory committees provide an important layer of independent review.
Dr. William Moss, director of the International Vaccine Access Center, stated that skipping advisory committee meetings removes experts from the conversation.
Decisions regarding vaccines and diseases are tricky and nuanced, according to Dr. William Moss.
Open discussion among experts familiar with vaccines and diseases is critically important, according to Dr. William Moss.
The FDA has not specified how it will select vaccine strains for the upcoming fall.
Following WHO's recommendations would mean the US relies on WHO data.
President Donald Trump previously ordered the US to withdraw support from WHO and cease information sharing.

**********Claims for hard query**********

The World Health Organization’s Global Influenza Surveillance and Response System is a network of seven collaborating centers and four essential regulatory labs.
ACIP typically meets three times a year, in February, June, and October.
The 97% figure referred to an audit of conflict of interest forms submitted by CDC advisers in 2007.

**********Formulated hard query**********

Something is a network of seven collaborating centers and four essential regulatory labs.
ACIP typically meets three times a year, at certain times.
The 97% figure referred to an audit of conflict of interest forms submitted by CDC advisers in a certain year.

**********Claims for medium query**********

Both the CDC and FDA are members of the World Health Organization’s Global Influenza Surveillance and Response System.
The selection of flu vaccine strains is carefully orchestrated to ensure timely delivery to manufacturers.
The WHO announced its recommended flu strains.

**********Formulated medium query**********

Both the CDC and FDA are members of `a certain system`.
The selection of flu vaccine strains is done `in a certain way` to ensure timely delivery to manufacturers.
The WHO announced `something`.

**********Claims for easy query**********

Dr. Tina Tan, president of the Infectious Diseases Society of America, stated that canceling the FDA meeting may prevent vaccine makers from having vital information and time to produce and distribute targeted vaccines before the next flu season.
Two long-planned vaccine meetings, one at the US Centers for Disease Control (CDC) and another at the US Food and Drug Administration (FDA), have been disrupted.
Dr. William Moss, director of the International Vaccine Access Center, stated that skipping advisory committee meetings removes experts from the conversation.

**********Formulated easy query**********

Someone, president of the Infectious Diseases Society of America, stated that canceling the FDA meeting may prevent vaccine makers from having vital information and time to produce and distribute targeted vaccines before the next flu season.
Two long-planned vaccine meetings, one at a certain organization and another at the US Food and Drug Administration (FDA), have been disrupted.
Dr. William Moss, director of the International Vaccine Access Center, stated that in a certain way removes experts from the conversation.

