Title: He tried building smartphones in the US over a decade ago. He has advice for companies trying it today
URL: https://edition.cnn.com/2025/08/04/tech/smartphones-made-in-the-us-motorola-tariffs?utm_source=business_ribbon
Content: # He tried building smartphones in the US over a decade ago. He has advice for companies trying it today

In 2013, Motorola tried to claw its way into a bigger share of a smartphone market dominated by Apple and Samsung with four words: Made in the USA.



**********Extracted claims**********

Motorola attempted to gain a larger share of the smartphone market in 2013.
The smartphone market in 2013 was dominated by Apple and Samsung.
Motorola's 2013 attempt to increase market share involved the phrase "Made in the USA."
Dennis Woodside served as the CEO of Motorola.
Dennis Woodside is the current CEO of Freshworks, an enterprise software service provider.
A segment of customers stated they were more likely to consider products produced in the United States.
Motorola's efforts to produce products in the United States were short-lived.
Motorola closed its Texas factory in 2014.
Motorola stopped domestic assembly of the Moto X.
The Moto X was Motorola's flagship phone at that time.
The Moto X was intended to compete with the latest iPhone and Samsung Galaxy device.
Dennis Woodside's experience highlights reasons why many tech products like smartphones are primarily assembled in Asia and South America rather than in America.
Proximity to crucial suppliers and lower labor costs are partial reasons for this manufacturing trend.
A gap in necessary skills and difficulty in filling factory jobs make it challenging to bring smartphone production to the United States.
The manufacturing challenges Motorola faced over a decade ago are currently relevant.
President Donald Trump is pressuring Apple and Samsung to manufacture their mobile devices in the United States.
Apple and Samsung face tariffs if they do not produce their mobile devices in the United States.
Higher tariffs on imports from China are set to take effect on August 12.
Many electronics are assembled in China.
India is currently the world's top exporter of smartphones to the United States.
India will face 25% tariffs starting August 7.
Dennis Woodside managed Motorola when it was owned by Google from May 2012 until its sale to Lenovo in 2014.
Companies attempting to make smartphones in America today should not underestimate the difficulty of finding and retaining skilled workers.
Companies need a strong value proposition for employees.
Companies should be thoughtful about using automation.
Companies must manage all economic aspects to ensure price competitiveness in the market.
Motorola, then owned by Google, made an ambitious decision in 2013 to produce its Moto X smartphone in Fort Worth, Texas.
Producing the Moto X in the US appealed to American shoppers who desired domestically made products.
Domestic production enabled Motorola to offer more customization for the Moto X than available with the latest iPhone or Galaxy phone.
Consumers could customize details like button and back panel color for the Moto X through Motorola's website.
The ability to customize was a key selling point of the Moto X.
Manufacturing closer to the consumer was necessary for offering customization.
Motorola assembled Moto X units sold in the US in Texas.
Components for the Moto X, including the battery, screen, and motherboard, were sourced from Asian suppliers.
The Moto X did not sell sufficiently to sustain its assembly in the United States.
Strategy Analytics reported that 500,000 Moto X units were sold in the third quarter of 2013.
By May 2014, Motorola confirmed plans to close the Fort Worth factory.
Motorola also planned to assemble the Moto X in other locations after closing the Fort Worth factory.
Higher costs were a challenge for Motorola's domestic manufacturing efforts.
Motorola faced a very fragmented supply chain.
Motorola's efforts may represent the only attempt to build smartphones in America at scale.
Tech startup Purism manufactures its Liberty phone in America.
Purism's manufacturing scale is not comparable to Motorola's scale at that time.
Motorola's Texas facility assembled approximately 100,000 phones per week.
Motorola needed to manufacture millions of phones annually due to its market.
Training and retaining employees was one of the biggest challenges encountered by Dennis Woodside.
Workers had alternative job options, such as positions in retail or food service.
The availability of other job options made it difficult to attract and retain staff for Motorola.
The specific nature of the work made attracting and retaining staff more difficult.
A smartphone contains several hundred tiny parts.
Most people in the United States were not accustomed to the type of work required for smartphone assembly.
Motorola had to train people for the specific type of work involved in smartphone assembly.
Dennis Woodside's experience highlights America's skill and demand shortage.
America's skill and demand shortage makes it difficult to fill factory jobs.
America's skill and demand shortage makes it difficult to onshore the production of tech products like smartphones.
The US manufacturing sector experienced an estimated loss of 11,000 jobs between June and July.
This job loss data comes from the US Bureau of Labor Statistics.
The job loss in manufacturing between June and July is lower than the approximately 15,000 manufacturing positions eliminated between May and June.
The manufacturing sector is one of the hardest-hit sectors in terms of job cuts for the past month.
Donald Trump's tariff policies have made it challenging to create new factory jobs.
There is evidence suggesting that factories struggled to find suitable workers prior to Trump's tariff policies.
There is evidence suggesting that Americans may not desire factory jobs.
A survey by the Cato Institute in August 2024 found that most Americans disagreed with the statement: "I would be better off if I worked in a factory instead of my current field of work."
Attracting and retaining a quality workforce was identified as a top business challenge for US manufacturers.
This challenge was reported in the National Association of Manufacturers outlook survey from the first quarter of this year.
The situation in China differs from that in the United States regarding manufacturing.
China has a plentiful labor market for assembling smartphones.
China's manufacturing sector has been expanding significantly.
Approximately 123 million people were employed in manufacturing in China in 2023.
Manufacturing was China's largest industry by employment in 2023.
This data is from China's fifth economic census, published in December 2024.
China hosts Foxconn's large Zhengzhou facility.
Foxconn is an Apple partner.
Approximately 350 iPhones were assembled per minute at Foxconn's Zhengzhou facility in 2016.
Apple has moved some of its manufacturing to India and Vietnam.
Apple's relocation of manufacturing aims to reduce its dependence on China.
India has become the top exporter of phones to the United States for the first time.
Apple CEO Tim Cook described China's workforce as ideal for smartphone manufacturing in 2017.
Tim Cook stated that China offers a combination of "craftsman" skills, "sophisticated robotics," and "the computer science world."
Smartphone assembly involves mounting small components such as camera parts and chips onto a device.
This task requires significant dexterity to perform throughout the day.
China's method of developing talent for manufacturing jobs differs from that of the United States.
In the United States, technical training and vocational education vary by state or industry.
The variation in technical training and vocational education in the United States can make it difficult to develop a qualified workforce at scale.
Sujai Shivakumar is the director and fellow at the Center for Strategic and International Studies.
Most smartphones globally are manufactured in China.
Smartphones manufactured in India hold a slightly higher market share among US customers compared to those from China.
China's share of the global smartphone manufacturing market was 67% in Q1 2025.
China's share of the US smartphone market by manufacturing country was 35% in the first half of the year.
India's share of the global smartphone manufacturing market was 17% in Q1 2025.
India's share of the US smartphone market by manufacturing country was 40% in the first half of the year.
Other countries accounted for 11% of the global smartphone manufacturing market in Q1 2025.
Other countries accounted for 25% of the US smartphone market by manufacturing country in the first half of the year.
Global smartphone manufacturing data is from Q1 2025.
US smartphone manufacturing data is from the first half of the year.
Data for other countries in the chart is rounded.
Sujai Shivakumar stated that a workforce is as crucial as electricity and transportation infrastructure for a manufacturing strategy.
China anticipates the need for a workforce and incorporates it into its planning.
Many new manufacturing jobs will likely require new skills, including coding and data analytics.
Artificial intelligence and automation will have a larger role in factories.
Carolyn Lee is the executive director of the Manufacturing Institute.
Dennis Woodside advises companies considering manufacturing electronics in the United States to carefully assess their ability to find staff with the necessary skills.
Companies should consider whether they will need to fully train the workforce to understand specific products.

