Title: Did the Fed just royally screw up?
URL: https://edition.cnn.com/2025/08/02/economy/fed-rate-cuts-jobs
Content: # Did the Fed just royally screw up?

—

It took only a few days for the Federal Reserve’s latest decision on interest rates to age like milk.

The central bank on Wednesday said it was holding borrowing costs steady yet again, extending a wait-and-see pa

**********Extracted claims**********

The Federal Reserve, the central bank, stated on Wednesday that it was holding borrowing costs steady.
Holding borrowing costs steady extended a wait-and-see pattern that began in January.
On Wednesday, Fed Chair Jerome Powell told reporters that a solid labor market meant central bankers could wait to observe the impact of President Donald Trump’s tariffs on prices before resuming rate cuts.
Rate cuts could help boost jobs.
Rate cuts could reignite inflation.
Two days after the Federal Reserve's decision, the job market appeared shakier than Fed Chair Jerome Powell had suggested.
On Friday, the Labor Department reported that employers added 73,000 jobs in July.
The reported July job growth of 73,000 was below the threshold of monthly job growth necessary to keep up with population growth.
The unemployment rate increased to 4.2% from 4.1%.
The Labor Department revised downward the job gains for the two months prior to July.
Based on newly revised data, job growth has been anemic.
The average pace of monthly job growth from May through July was the weakest for any three-month period since 2009, excluding the 2020 pandemic recession.
The Federal Reserve declined CNN’s request for comment.
Jamie Cox, managing partner at Harris Financial Group, stated that Fed Chair Jerome Powell would regret holding rates steady during that week.
The Federal Reserve’s latest decision generated internal pushback on a scale not seen in decades.
Fed Governor Christopher Waller cast a dissenting vote regarding the latest Fed decision.
Fed Vice Chair for Supervision Michelle Bowman cast a dissenting vote regarding the latest Fed decision.
This marked the first time that more than one Fed governor cast a dissenting vote since 1993.
In statements issued on Friday, Fed Governor Christopher Waller and Fed Vice Chair Michelle Bowman cited signs of weakness in the labor market as a major reason for their dissent.
Fed Governor Christopher Waller and Fed Vice Chair Michelle Bowman downplayed the potential effects of President Trump’s tariffs on prices.
Congress tasks the Fed with addressing both high inflation and a weakening labor market.
Michelle Bowman wrote that the labor market has become less dynamic and shows increasing signs of fragility.
Michelle Bowman stated that few industries have propelled job growth this year.
