Title: Giant shoes found near Hadrian’s Wall spark mystery around the soldiers of ancient Rome
URL: https://edition.cnn.com/2025/07/12/science/giant-shoes-ancient-roman-fort-magna
Content: # Giant shoes found near Hadrian’s Wall spark mystery around the soldiers of ancient Rome

—

An ancient Roman mystery is afoot in the rolling hills of northern Britain.

Archaeologists have unearthed a stash of unusually large shoes at the ruins of a fir

**********Extracted claims**********

Archaeologists have unearthed a stash of unusually large shoes at the ruins of a first-century military fort along Hadrian’s Wall.
Hadrian’s Wall is a 73-mile (117-kilometer) stone barrier.
Hadrian’s Wall famously shielded the Roman Empire’s northwestern perimeter from foreign invaders.
The discovery of the shoes is raising new questions about the lives and origins of the fort’s inhabitants.
Giant leather soles were found at Magna Fort in May.
34 pieces of footwear were found at Magna Fort.
The footwear found at Magna Fort includes work boots and baby-sized shoes.
The footwear found at Magna Fort is helping to paint a picture of the 4,000 men, women, and children who once lived in and around the English site.
Magna Fort is located just south of the Scottish border.
Eight of the shoes found at Magna Fort are over 11.8 inches (30 centimeters) in length.
An 11.8-inch (30-centimeter) shoe length corresponds to a US men’s size 13.5 or greater according to Nike’s size chart.
The shoes found at Magna Fort are larger than average by today’s standard.
The large shoe size has sparked suspicions that unusually tall troops may have guarded the fortress at the empire’s edge.
The average ancient shoe found at a neighboring Roman fort was closer to a US men’s size 8.
A news release about the discovery provided information about the average ancient shoe size at a neighboring Roman fort.
Rachel Frame is a senior archaeologist leading the excavation at Magna Fort.
Rachel Frame believes the large shoes were worn by people with really large feet.
Digging continues at Magna Fort.
A basic sketch of Magna Fort’s past is just starting to come together.
Magna Fort was built around AD 85.
Archaeologists suspect multiple different Roman military troops and their families moved into Magna Fort every few years when it was in use.
Inscriptions on Magna Fort’s walls and altars recount settlements of Hamian archers from what is now Syria.
Inscriptions on Magna Fort’s walls and altars recount settlements of Dalmatian mountain soldiers from Croatia and Serbia.
Inscriptions on Magna Fort’s walls and altars recount settlements of Batavians from the Netherlands.
The length of time each group stayed at the Magna Fort stronghold remains unknown.
Troops at Magna Fort would often leave the fort for distant regions.
Troops at Magna Fort ditched shoes, clothing, and other belongings in the surrounding trenches.
New occupants at Magna Fort would have built larger structures on top of the existing fort.
New occupants at Magna Fort packed rubble and clay between the walls.
The packing of rubble and clay trapped belongings left by previous tenants at Magna Fort.
Dr. Elizabeth Greene is an associate professor of classics at the University of Western Ontario.
Dr. Elizabeth Greene has studied thousands of shoes collected from the Vindolanda Roman Fort.
Vindolanda Roman Fort is located near Magna Fort.
Vindolanda Roman Fort has been excavated since the 1970s.
Vindolanda Roman Fort is among the most well-studied Roman forts along Hadrian’s Wall.
The recently discovered Magna shoes share similarities with shoes in the Vindolanda Fort collection.
Dr. Elizabeth Greene was not involved in the Magna excavation process.
Dr. Elizabeth Greene has viewed the Magna artifacts.
The soles of the shoes from both Magna and Vindolanda sites are made from thick layers of cowhide leather.
The soles of the shoes from both Magna and Vindolanda sites are held together with iron hobnails.
Only a couple of the shoes discovered at Magna Fort have some of the upper portions still intact.
Vindolanda Fort shoe styles include closed military boots.
Vindolanda Fort shoe styles include open work boots.
Vindolanda Fort shoe styles include sneaker-like shoes reaching just below the ankle.
Vindolanda Fort shoe styles include sandals with leather fasteners.
The leather soles of the Magna shoes likely survived thousands of years in the ground due to ancient tanning techniques.
Ancient tanning techniques used crushed up vegetative matter.
Ancient tanning techniques created a water and heat resistant coating.
Testing is still underway to confirm the hypothesis about tanning techniques used on the Magna shoes.
The length of the extra-large shoes found at Magna Fort suggests the original owners may have been exceptionally tall.
At Vindolanda, only 16 out of 3,704 collected shoes measured over 11.8 inches (30 centimeters).
Ancient Roman military manuals often described the ideal recruit as being 5 feet, 8 inches or 5 feet, 9 inches in height.
Rob Collins is a professor of frontier archaeology at Newcastle University in England.
Soldiers stationed around Hadrian’s Wall came from all around the far-reaching Roman Empire.
Soldiers stationed around Hadrian’s Wall brought a wide diversity of physical traits to their settlements.
The reason why Magna Fort might have needed troops of towering stature remains unclear.
Researchers will examine the Magna shoes for any signs of wear to piece together the shoe owners’ identities.
Foot impressions left in the shoes could be used to model the feet of the original wearers.
Linking the shoes to real human remains could prove difficult.
The Romans near Hadrian’s Wall generally cremated their dead.
The Romans near Hadrian’s Wall used a headstone to mark graves.
Any bones remaining around Roman settlements near Hadrian’s Wall are likely from enemy, illegal, or accidental burials.
Bones found at the Magna site were too soft and crumbly to provide insight.
The team continues to search for new burial spots at the Magna site.
Pottery and other artifacts found around Magna Fort may help with dating the site.
Pottery and other artifacts found around Magna Fort may help with matching the timelines of known occupants.
Researchers are concerned about running out of time for excavations.
Leather found at both the Vindolanda and Magna sites is 2,000 years old.
Leather found at both the Vindolanda and Magna sites is preserved by anaerobic (low-oxygen) conditions of the soil.
The 34 shoes found at Magna Fort are in worse condition than shoes retrieved from Vindolanda decades ago.
Rachel Frame attributes the worse condition of the Magna shoes to the changing climate.
Climate change leads to more heat waves and droughts.
Climate change leads to scenarios like months’ worth of rain in one weekend.
Climate change influences underground soil conditions.
Climate change introduces more oxygen into underground environments.
In oxygen-rich soil, microbes thrive.
Microbes contribute to decay in oxygen-rich soil.
Acidic pH levels erode natural materials like leather.
Rapid weather changes make the excavation of Magna Fort more urgent.

