Title: What you eat may impact your lung cancer risk, according to a new study
URL: https://edition.cnn.com/2025/07/29/health/ultraprocessed-foods-lung-cancer-wellness
Content: # What you eat may impact your lung cancer risk, according to a new study

Video 
\- Source:

There are many reasons to avoid ultraprocessed foods, including a link with heart disease, diabetes and obesity,but an increased risk of lung cancer may be yet a

**********Extracted claims**********

A new study suggests that dietary intake may influence an individual's lung cancer risk.
An increased risk of lung cancer may be linked to the consumption of ultraprocessed foods.
Ultraprocessed foods are associated with an increased risk of heart disease, diabetes, and obesity.
According to the Food and Agriculture Organization of the United Nations, ultraprocessed foods contain ingredients "never or rarely used in kitchens, or classes of additives whose function is to make the final product palatable or more appealing."
Ingredients found in ultraprocessed foods can include preservatives against mold or bacteria, artificial coloring, emulsifiers to prevent separation, and added or altered sugar, salt, and fats to enhance appeal.
Ultraprocessed foods include items such as sodas, chips, packaged soups, chicken nuggets, and ice cream.
People who consume the most ultraprocessed foods are 41% more likely to be diagnosed with lung cancer than those who consume the least.
This increased likelihood of lung cancer diagnosis among consumers of the most ultraprocessed foods remains consistent even after accounting for other factors like smoking.
The study linking ultraprocessed foods and lung cancer risk was published in the journal Thorax.
Researchers analyzed data from more than 100,000 individuals for the study.
The data analyzed included information from the National Health and Nutrition Examination Survey’s Food Frequency Questionnaire on dietary habits.
Researchers compared the dietary habit information with medical records for lung cancer diagnoses.
On average, study participants consumed nearly three servings of ultraprocessed foods daily.
The most commonly consumed ultraprocessed foods in the study were lunch meat, diet or caffeinated soft drinks, or decaffeinated soft drinks.
Industrial food processing alters the food matrix.
Altering the food matrix affects nutrient availability and absorption.
Industrial food processing generates harmful contaminants.
Acrolein is a harmful contaminant that can originate from burning tobacco, wood, plastics, and gasoline.
Acrolein can originate from cooking fats and oils at high temperatures, according to the US Centers for Disease Control and Prevention.
The study on ultraprocessed foods and lung cancer is observational.
An observational study cannot definitively prove that certain foods cause lung cancer.
An observational study can indicate a connection between foods and lung cancer.
Lung cancer is a common form of cancer.
The World Health Organization estimated 2.4 million new cases of lung cancer globally in 2022.
Smoking is the primary contributor to lung cancer risk.
Individuals who do not smoke can still develop lung cancer.
Factors other than smoking contribute to lung cancer risk.
The authors of the study adjusted for other potential factors that could increase lung cancer diagnoses, including a person's smoking status.
The measurements used for smoking adjustment in the study were not nuanced, according to Dr. Fang Fang Zhang.
The association between ultraprocessed food consumption and lung cancer risk was stronger among never-smokers.
Limited research exists specifically on the link between ultraprocessed foods and lung cancer risk.
Extensive existing evidence demonstrates the importance of diet quality in predicting premature death from all causes in the United States.
Consumption of ultraprocessed foods is often linked to a lower overall diet quality.
Lower diet quality includes a higher intake of saturated fats, chemicals, salt, and sugar.
Lower diet quality also includes higher calorie consumption.
These dietary factors can drive inflammation.
Inflammation is a primary pathway in the development and progression of cancer.
These dietary factors can damage the microbiome, which impairs immune system function.
A combination of excess inflammation and impaired immunity gives rogue cells an advantage.
Cancer initiation occurs in scenarios characterized by excess inflammation and impaired immunity.
Ultraprocessed foods tend to have low levels of omega-3s.
Omega-3s are essential healthy fats that the body cannot produce on its own, according to the National Institutes of Health Office of Dietary Supplements.
Omega-3s limit the shelf life of food products.
The study contributes lung cancer risk to the list of reasons to avoid ultraprocessed foods.
The study reinforces the importance of improving overall diet quality.
Health and diet quality improve when people primarily consume whole, unprocessed foods.
Health and diet quality improve when diets largely consist of plants such such as vegetables, fruits, whole grains, beans, lentils, nuts, and seeds.

