Title: Trump’s tariffs are sending African countries into China’s hands
URL: https://edition.cnn.com/2025/08/02/africa/trumps-tariffs-africa-and-china-intl
Content: # Trump’s tariffs are sending African countries into China’s hands

Africa is adjusting to the new reality of US President Donald Trump’s tariffs, with countries on the continent facing some of the highest export charges.

But what could become a crisis i

**********Extracted claims**********

African countries are adjusting to the reality of US President Donald Trump’s tariffs.
African countries are facing some of the highest export charges due to US President Donald Trump's tariffs.
Nigerian economist Bismarck Rewane stated that Africa is moving directly into China's influence.
China has emerged as Africa's largest bilateral trading partner in recent years.
Libya, South Africa, Algeria, and Tunisia face export tariffs from the Trump administration ranging from 25% to 30%.
Eighteen other African countries were subjected to 15% levies under a modified tariff package released by the White House.
In April, when US import levies were first announced, Trump described them as "reciprocal."
Trump stated that the US import levies targeted countries with trade deficits with the US.
Trump based his tariffs on countries' trade deficits with the United States.
South Africa challenged the imposition of a 30% tariff on its exports to the US.
South Africa stated that Trump's decision on tariffs was not based on an accurate representation of available trade data.
China offered to mitigate the impact of US tariffs on Africa.
China stated in June that it would stop charges on imports for nearly all its African partners.
South African researcher Neo Letswalo stated there is no better opportunity for African countries to strengthen South-South trade.
Letswalo stated that America is gradually losing its global leadership status.
Letswalo added that reduced dependence on the US by countries creates a greater opportunity for China to become an alternative.
The US did not make a trade deal with any African nation before the tariff deadline.
Letswalo described America’s failure to negotiate a deal with Africa as "an open goal for China."
The impact of Trump’s tariffs is already being felt in some of Africa’s strongest and poorest economies.
Lesotho was subjected to a 15% tariff.
Lesotho had previously faced a 50% tariff before the charges were modified.
Lesotho’s Prime Minister Samuel Matekane stated in June that the large tariff and the halt of US aid have crippled industries that previously supported thousands of jobs in Lesotho.
Trump described Lesotho as a country "nobody has ever heard of."
Trade between the US and Lesotho totaled over $240 million in the previous year.
Most of the trade between the US and Lesotho was in textiles.
Lesotho is a landlocked nation surrounded by South Africa.
Before the tariffs, Lesotho and other eligible sub-Saharan countries could export goods to the US duty-free under a US trade agreement.
Authorities in Lesotho have declared a two-year national state of disaster due to the tariffs.
Lesotho's textile industry is already experiencing significant job losses.
Thousands of jobs are threatened in South Africa.
Citrus growers in South Africa expressed great anxiety before the August 1 tariff deadline.
South Africa’s Citrus Growers’ Association (CGA) warned that job losses would be certain if tariffs became effective.
Hundreds of thousands of cartons of citrus are prepared in South African packhouses for shipment to the US.
Implementing the charges would result in most of this fruit remaining unsold.
Analysts stated that other South African industries, including the automobile sector, face the risk of economic shocks.
Letswalo stated that companies within South Africa's automobile sector are threatening to leave due to plummeting business.
Gwede Mantashe, South Africa’s minister of mineral and petroleum resources, stated that alternative routes are being sought for South African goods.
