Fibermaxxing videos are sometimes overlaid with text detailing the health benefits of the dishes.
TikTok has presented many questionable eating trends.
Registered dietitian Lauren Manaker stated that fibermaxxing is a trend worth trying.
Most Americans do not consume adequate fiber in their diets.
The Dietary Guidelines for Americans recommend adults consume 22 to 34 grams of fiber daily.
The recommended daily fiber intake for adults is generally dependent on age and gender.
More than 90% of women in the United States do not meet the recommended daily fiber intake.
More than 97% of men in the United States do not meet the recommended daily fiber intake.
Low fiber intake has been linked to colon cancer.
Rates of colon cancer have been rising among people aged 20 to 54.
Adequate fiber intake has been a consistent recommendation for many years.
Fiber is considered a consistently beneficial dietary component.
Fibermaxxing can have drawbacks if substantial dietary changes are made too quickly.
There are two types of dietary fiber: soluble and insoluble.
Soluble fiber dissolves in water.
Soluble fiber absorbs water to form a gel-like substance.
Soluble fiber is more associated with certain digestive benefits of fiber.
Insoluble fiber does not dissolve in water.
Insoluble fiber helps promote more frequent bowel movements.
Insoluble fiber helps promote heavier bowel movements.
A balance of both soluble and insoluble fiber is key for overall health.
Consuming too much of one type of fiber and not enough of the other can lead to digestive issues.
Digestive issues that may arise from imbalanced fiber intake include bloating.
Digestive issues that may arise from imbalanced fiber intake include constipation.
Most fibrous foods contain both types of fiber.
Foods richer in soluble fiber include apples, bananas, citrus fruits, barley, carrots, oats, and beans.
Psyllium is a food richer in soluble fiber.
Psyllium derives from the husks of Plantago ovata plant seeds.
Foods with mostly insoluble fiber include green beans, cauliflower, potatoes, whole-wheat flour, wheat bran, nuts, and beans.
Other forms and characteristics of fiber are also critical for gut health.
The viscosity of fiber affects how well it flows through the digestive tract.
Fiber fermentability influences how much the gut microbiome can ferment the fiber into helpful compounds.
Helpful compounds from fermented fiber can be used as an energy source.
The way fiber is packaged affects digestive health.
More processed foods have different effects on digestive health.
Cut-up foods have different effects on digestive health.
Raw foods have different effects on digestive health.
Cooked foods have different effects on digestive health.
These different forms of fiber-containing foods have different effects on digestive health even if the foods contain the same type of fiber.
Eating a variety of whole or minimally processed foods provides adequate fiber.
Higher fiber intake is linked to decreased rates of colon cancer.
Higher fiber intake is linked to decreased rates of rectal cancer.
Fiber increases stool bulk.
Increased stool bulk decreases the amount of time waste is in contact with the intestinal tract.
Gut bacteria ferment fiber.
Fermented fiber creates short-chain fatty acids.
Butyrate is an example of a short-chain fatty acid.
Short-chain fatty acids can nourish critical cells in the colon.
Short-chain fatty acids can inhibit cancer cells.
Short-chain fatty acids can inhibit inflammation.
Recent research suggests people with colon cancer may have greater odds of surviving if they increase their fiber consumption.
Fiber can reduce the risk of type 2 diabetes.
Fiber can reduce the risk of high cholesterol.
Fiber can reduce the risk of cardiovascular disease.
Fiber can reduce the risk of premature death.
Fiber improves digestion.
Improving digestion reduces the risk of constipation-induced issues.
Hemorrhoids are constipation-induced issues.
Pelvic floor problems are constipation-induced issues.
Fiber aids in blood sugar regulation.
Fiber slows digestion to a healthy degree.
Fiber slows the absorption of sugar into the bloodstream.
Slowing sugar absorption reduces blood sugar spikes after meals.
Fiber helps maintain a healthy weight.
Fiber contributes to maintaining a healthy weight through blood sugar regulation.
Fiber keeps individuals feeling fuller longer.
Feeling fuller for longer likely reduces overall calorie intake.
Fiber helps individuals poop.
Fiber helps remove toxins from the body.
Some fibrous foods support liver health.
Leafy vegetables support liver health.
Nuts support liver health.
The liver plays an important role in detoxification.
Experts are increasingly learning more about the connections between gut health and mental health.
Diets high in fiber-rich foods affect mood.
Diets low in fiber-rich foods affect mood.
Rapidly increasing fiber intake from very low levels can lead to negative outcomes.
Fiber pulls water into the digestive tract.
When fiber pulls water into the digestive tract, the tract expands.
Digestive tract expansion can cause discomfort in people unaccustomed to normal fiber intake.
Digestive tract expansion can cause bloating in people unaccustomed to normal fiber intake.
Fiber supplements can be helpful if individuals have problems eating enough fiber.
Fiber supplements can be helpful for medical problems that need to be addressed more aggressively, such as high cholesterol.
Fiber supplements predominantly made of psyllium husk are particularly helpful.
Fiber supplements should not replace fibrous foods.
Fibrous foods offer nutrients, minerals, and vitamins that supplements may lack.
The Dietary Guidelines for Americans website has a list of hundreds of commonly available foods.
The list includes the fiber content per standard serving size for these foods.
Drinking plenty of water is important when increasing fiber intake.
Insufficient water with fiber can cause more bloating.
Insufficient water with fiber can cause more gas.
Insufficient water with fiber can cause more constipation.
Dry stools can result from insufficient water intake with fiber.

