Title: Scientists recover proteins from a 24 million-year-old rhino fossil. Are dinosaurs next?
URL: https://edition.cnn.com/2025/07/15/science/ancient-protein-rhino-fossil
Content: # Scientists recover proteins from a 24 million-year-old rhino fossil. Are dinosaurs next?

—

Scientists have recovered ancient proteins from a fossilized rhinoceros tooth, breaking new ground in the study of ancient life on Earth.

The 24 million-year-o

**********Extracted claims**********

Scientists have recovered ancient proteins from a fossilized rhinoceros tooth.
The recovery of ancient proteins from a fossilized rhinoceros tooth breaks new ground in the study of ancient life on Earth.
A 24-million-year-old fossilized rhinoceros tooth was unearthed in the Canadian Arctic.
The 24-million-year-old rhinoceros tooth contains proteins.
The proteins found in the 24-million-year-old rhinoceros tooth are 10 times older than the most ancient known DNA.
Scientists have analyzed the oldest detailed protein sequence on record using the sample from the rhinoceros tooth.
Ryan Sinclair Paterson stated that enamel is hard and protects proteins over long time scales.
Ryan Sinclair Paterson is a postdoctoral researcher at the Globe Institute at the University of Copenhagen in Denmark.
Ryan Sinclair Paterson led the Canadian research.
The study of ancient DNA preserved in bones, fossils, and dirt has revolutionized archaeological science.
Ancient DNA has provided information about lost empires, mysterious clans, ice age creatures, and previously unknown human species.
Ancient proteins hold the potential for a revolution in the study of fossils that are many millions of years old.
Fossils that are many millions of years old are currently beyond the chronological reach of ancient DNA.
The study on the rhino tooth was published on July 9 in the scientific journal Nature.
The study showcases the enormous potential of the field known as paleoproteomics.
The field of studying ancient proteins is known as paleoproteomics.
Proteins are made up of sequences of amino acids.
Proteins are more robust than DNA.
DNA is a fragile molecule that degrades relatively easily.
Proteins contain less detailed information than DNA.
Proteins can help to elucidate a specimen’s evolutionary history.
Proteins can help to elucidate a specimen’s diet.
Proteins can help determine the sex of a fossil in some cases.
Enrico Cappellini is a coauthor of the study.
Enrico Cappellini is a professor at the University of Copenhagen’s Globe Institute.
Enrico Cappellini has pioneered methods to extricate proteins from fossils.
Enrico Cappellini was involved in the Canadian research.
Cappellini, Paterson, and colleagues at the University of York and the Canadian Museum of Nature recovered sequences from seven proteins.
The seven proteins recovered were preserved inside the fossilized rhino tooth.
Sequencing ancient proteins involves determining the order of amino acids in a sample.
Scientists can glean information about the evolution of a rhino by comparing its protein sequences with those of living and extinct relatives.
The analysis of the rhino protein sequences revealed that the rhino diverged from the same family as living rhinos approximately 41 million to 25 million years ago.
Ryan Sinclair Paterson stated that the fossil record includes various rhinoceros species forms, such as the woolly rhinoceros and the Siberian unicorn.
Ryan Sinclair Paterson stated that researchers were able to compare the mystery rhino with other forms to determine its place in the family tree.
Separate research was also published on July 9 in the journal Nature.
The separate research sampled fossils from Kenya’s Turkana Basin.
The separate research suggests biomolecules can survive for millions of years in searing, tropical environments.
The Kenyan study analyzed 10 mammal fossils.
The mammal fossils analyzed in the Kenyan study included relatives of modern elephants, hippos, and rhinos.
The Kenyan study was published by researchers at the Smithsonian Institution’s Museum Conservation Institute and Harvard University.
Proteins were recovered from five of the Kenyan fossils.
The five Kenyan fossils from which proteins were recovered are dated between 1.5 million and 18 million years ago.
Scientists can extract prehistoric proteins even in tropical regions with high temperatures.
Prehistoric proteins can reveal links between ancient elephants and rhinos and their modern-day relatives.
The authors of the Kenyan study stated that the information in the Kenyan proteins was less detailed than that found in the Canadian fossil.
The authors of the Kenyan study stated that the presence of proteins within enamel tissues in one of the world’s warmest regions holds promise that proteins in much older fossils could be discovered.
Timothy Cleland is a study author.
Timothy Cleland is a physical scientist at the Museum Conservation Institute.
Maarten Dhaenens is a researcher at the University of Ghent in Belgium.
Maarten Dhaenens specializes in proteomics.
Maarten Dhaenens stated that the research on the Canadian fossil was "sound and super interesting."
Maarten Dhaenens stated that the methodology used on the Kenyan fossils was complex and less tested.
Maarten Dhaenens argued that the Kenyan researchers’ findings are harder to interpret and warranted a more thorough assessment.
Maarten Dhaenens stated that the data from the Kenyan study is publicly available.
Evan Saitta is a paleontologist and research associate at Chicago’s Field Museum of Natural History.
Evan Saitta stated that finding proteins preserved within fossils at tropical latitudes was "shocking."
Evan Saitta stated that the findings from tropical latitudes needed replication.
It was previously assumed that cold temperatures were necessary to slow down the breakdown of proteins.
Matthew Collins is the McDonald Professor in Palaeoproteomics at the UK’s University of Cambridge.
Matthew Collins stated that getting proteins from very old fossils would be a palaeontologist’s dream come true.
Matthew Collins agreed that the research on the Canadian fossil was more convincing.
Matthew Collins has tried to recover proteins from dinosaur fossils.
Collins and Saitta were part of a team that detected amino acids in a titanosaur eggshell fragment.
Research detecting amino acids in a titanosaur eggshell fragment was published in 2024.
The titanosaur egg was laid by a plant-eating sauropod.
Sauropods were huge, long-necked dinosaurs.
Sauropods lived in the Late Cretaceous period.
Dinosaurs went extinct 66 million years ago.
The dinosaur eggshell lacked identifiable protein sequences.
Evan Saitta stated that the results from the dinosaur eggshell showed a pattern of decay.
Evan Saitta stated that the pattern of decay indicated there were once proteins in the eggshell.
Matthew Collins stated that there was no protein sequence left in the dinosaur eggshell.
Matthew Collins stated that only individual amino acids remained in the dinosaur eggshell.
Evan Saitta has given up looking for proteins in dinosaur fossils.
Evan Saitta stated that dinosaur fossils are far older than the fossils in the Canadian and Kenyan studies.
Evan Saitta stated that dinosaur fossils mostly date back to a hothouse period in the global climate.
Evan Saitta stated that there were no ice caps during the hothouse period when most dinosaur fossils date back to.
Evan Saitta stated that, on average, dinosaur fossils are buried far deeper.
Evan Saitta stated that dinosaur fossils have experienced far greater geothermal heat due to deeper burial.
Evan Saitta stated that it is not clear whether dinosaur teeth had thick enough enamel to preserve proteins.

