Cristen Brink returned to the location of the attack to attempt to save Clinton Brink.
State police Major Stacie Rhoads stated that Cristen Brink did not return completely to the car with her children.
Major Stacie Rhoads stated that investigators believe Cristen Brink took her children to safety before returning to help her husband.
The suspected attacker is identified as 28-year-old Andrew McGann.
Andrew McGann killed Cristen Brink and Clinton Brink.
The killings occurred as the daughters of Cristen Brink and Clinton Brink, aged 9 and 7, informed other adults at the park about the events.
Andrew McGann faces two counts of capital murder.
Washington County Prosecuting Attorney Brandon Carter stated that Cristen Brink and Clinton Brink protected their daughters to the fullest extent, resulting in their deaths.
A hiker on the trail observed the Brink children and escorted them to safety.
The hiker subsequently discovered the bodies of Cristen Brink and Clinton Brink.
A probable cause document filed by prosecutors provides details about the hiker's actions.
The hiker was delayed in calling 911 to report the deaths due to the absence of cell service in that part of the park.
Andrew McGann was taken into custody at a hair salon.
Andrew McGann's arrest occurred 30 miles from the initial location of a manhunt.
Andrew McGann's arrest occurred four days after the murders.
Andrew McGann admitted to the crimes during police questioning, according to state police.
Andrew McGann has no known adult criminal history.
Andrew McGann made a court appearance in Fayetteville.
Andrew McGann wore a striped jail uniform during his court appearance.
Andrew McGann spoke only to acknowledge his name in court.
A judge granted Andrew McGann a public defender.
A judge ordered Andrew McGann to remain jailed without bond.
Andrew McGann's arraignment is scheduled for August 25.
Investigators are still attempting to determine the motive for the killings.
Investigators are releasing more details about the circumstances of the crime.
Washington County Prosecuting Attorney Brandon Carter stated there is no reason to believe any known association existed between Andrew McGann and the victims.
Washington County Prosecuting Attorney Brandon Carter stated the event appears to be completely random.
Initially, the only description of the suspect available to police was provided by the Brink children.
Washington County Prosecuting Attorney Brandon Carter stated that all information investigators were using originated from the two young daughters of the victims.
Another witness observed a man leaving the trail with blood on his face.
The witness identified the suspected killer’s car as a Kia Stinger.
Investigators believe tape on the vehicle was designed to obscure its model.
A search of Arkansas registration records led investigators to identify Andrew McGann as their chief suspect, according to Major Stacie Rhoads.
Major Stacie Rhoads stated there is speculation that significant thought went into concealing Andrew McGann's identity.
Major Stacie Rhoads stated that Andrew McGann was also very sloppy.
The car identified in public advisories as the suspect's vehicle was parked at Lupita’s Beauty Salon & Barber Shop.
A lanky man with stringy blonde hair entered Lupita’s Beauty Salon & Barber Shop and requested a fade haircut.
Andrew McGann acknowledged the car was his when officers arrived, according to police.
Investigators observed several cuts on Andrew McGann's hands, as stated in a court document.
Surveillance video showed Andrew McGann did not resist while being handcuffed.
The salon shared surveillance video with CNN.
Major Stacie Rhoads stated that Andrew McGann was completely cooperative.
Andrew McGann’s car was searched.
Blood was found inside Andrew McGann’s car, according to a probable cause statement.
Andrew McGann made statements indicating he had committed the two homicides after receiving his Miranda warnings, according to a court document.
The motive for the killings of Clinton Brink, 43, and Cristen Brink, 41, remains unknown, according to investigators.
Andrew McGann had moved to Arkansas earlier in the year of the report.
Andrew McGann was scheduled to become an elementary teacher in the Springdale Public Schools, according to the district.
Andrew McGann had been hired as a teacher in at least five different school districts in Arkansas, Oklahoma, and Texas since 2022.
The school districts that hired Andrew McGann include Sand Springs Public Schools, Broken Arrow Public Schools, Lewisville Independent School District, Springdale Public Schools, and Plano Independent School District.
Andrew McGann did not accrue any classroom time with students in the Springdale school district.
Andrew McGann did not accrue any classroom time with students in the Plano school district.
