Title: He tried building smartphones in the US over a decade ago. He has advice for companies trying it today
URL: https://edition.cnn.com/2025/08/04/tech/smartphones-made-in-the-us-motorola-tariffs
Content: # He tried building smartphones in the US over a decade ago. He has advice for companies trying it today

In 2013, Motorola tried to claw its way into a bigger share of a smartphone market dominated by Apple and Samsung with four words: Made in the USA.



**********Extracted claims**********

In 2013, Motorola attempted to increase its share in the smartphone market.
Apple and Samsung dominated the smartphone market in 2013.
Motorola's 2013 strategy for smartphone market share involved emphasizing "Made in the USA."
A segment of customers indicated a preference for products manufactured in the United States.
Motorola closed its Texas factory in 2014.
Motorola stopped domestic assembly of the Moto X in 2014.
The Moto X was Motorola's flagship phone.
The Moto X was intended to compete with iPhones and Samsung Galaxy devices.
Proximity to crucial suppliers is a factor in smartphone assembly location.
Lower labor costs are a factor in smartphone assembly location.
A gap in necessary skills makes smartphone production challenging in the United States.
Difficulty in filling factory jobs makes smartphone production challenging in the United States.
President Donald Trump pressured Apple and Samsung to manufacture mobile devices in the United States.
Apple and Samsung faced the threat of tariffs if they did not manufacture mobile devices in the United States.
Higher tariffs on imports from China were set to take effect on August 12.
Many electronics are assembled in China.
The tariffs on imports from China could be averted if a deal or extension was reached.
India became the world’s top exporter of smartphones to the United States.
India was scheduled to face 25% tariffs on smartphones starting August 7.
Dennis Woodside was CEO of Motorola from May 2012 to 2014.
Google owned Motorola from May 2012 until its sale to Lenovo in 2014.
Google-owned Motorola began producing the Moto X smartphone in Fort Worth, Texas, in 2013.
Producing the Moto X in the US appealed to US shoppers who preferred domestically made products.
Manufacturing the Moto X in the US allowed for greater customization compared to iPhones or Galaxy phones.
Motorola offered consumers the ability to customize aesthetic details of the Moto X, such as button and back panel colors, via its website.
Customization was a key selling point for the Moto X.
Motorola assembled Moto X units for the US market in Texas.
Components for the Moto X, including the battery, screen, and motherboard, were sourced from Asian suppliers.
The Moto X did not achieve sufficient sales to sustain its US assembly.
Strategy Analytics reported 500,000 Moto X units were sold in the third quarter of 2013.
Motorola confirmed plans to close its Texas factory and relocate Moto X assembly by May 2014.
Higher costs were associated with Moto X production in the United States.
The supply chain for Moto X production was fragmented.
Motorola's Moto X production effort may be the only attempt to build smartphones in America at scale.
Tech startup Purism manufactures its Liberty phone in America.
Purism's production scale for the Liberty phone is much smaller than Motorola's Moto X production.
Motorola's Texas facility assembled approximately 100,000 phones per week.
Training and retaining employees were major challenges for Motorola's US smartphone production.
Workers in the US had alternative employment options, such as retail or food service jobs.
The availability of other job options made it difficult to attract and retain staff for smartphone manufacturing in the US.
Smartphones contain hundreds of tiny parts.
Most workers in the US were unaccustomed to the specific type of work required for smartphone assembly.
Motorola had to train workers for smartphone assembly in the US.
The United States faces a skill shortage in manufacturing.
The United States faces a demand shortage in manufacturing that makes it difficult to fill factory jobs.
The United States faces a demand shortage in manufacturing that makes it difficult to onshore production of tech products like smartphones.
The US manufacturing sector lost an estimated 11,000 jobs between June and July.
The US Bureau of Labor Statistics reported the loss of manufacturing jobs.
Approximately 15,000 manufacturing positions were eliminated between May and June.
The manufacturing sector was one of the hardest-hit sectors in terms of job cuts for the period reported.
A Cato Institute survey from August 2024 indicated that most Americans disagreed with the statement "I would be better off if I worked in a factory instead of my current field of work."
Attracting and retaining a quality workforce was identified as a top business challenge for US manufacturers in the National Association of Manufacturers outlook survey from the first quarter of the year.
China has a plentiful labor market for smartphone assembly.
China's manufacturing sector has experienced rapid growth.
Approximately 123 million people were employed in manufacturing in China in 2023.
Manufacturing employed the most people of any industry in China in 2023.
China's fifth economic census, published in December 2024, reported these employment figures for manufacturing.
China hosts Foxconn's Zhengzhou facility, an Apple partner.
Approximately 350 iPhones were assembled per minute at Foxconn's Zhengzhou facility in 2016.
Apple has moved some of its manufacturing operations to India.
Apple has moved some of its manufacturing operations to Vietnam.
Apple's manufacturing shift aimed to reduce reliance on China.
Tim Cook stated in 2017 that China's workforce for smartphone manufacturing offers a combination of "craftsman" skills, "sophisticated robotics," and "the computer science world."
Smartphone assembly involves mounting small components such as camera parts and chips onto a device.
Smartphone assembly requires significant dexterity for continuous work.
China's approach to developing manufacturing talent differs from the United States'.
Technical training and vocational education in the United States vary by state or industry.
Sujai Shivakumar, director and fellow at the Center for Strategic and International Studies, observed differences in manufacturing talent development approaches.
Sujai Shivakumar, director and fellow at the Center for Strategic and International Studies, stated that technical training and vocational education in the United States vary by state or industry.
China dominates the global smartphone manufacturing market.
Phones manufactured in India have a slightly higher market share among US customers.
Global smartphone manufacturing data cited is from Q1 2025.
US smartphone manufacturing data cited is from the first half of the year.
Carolyn Lee, executive director of the Manufacturing Institute, stated that many new manufacturing jobs will likely require skills such as coding and data analytics.
Artificial intelligence and automation are increasing their role in factories.

